__condition` and forwards `err-val` directly as the condition to `raise`.\n    48\t   - **Falsy**: calls `(u/app raise (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/thrown) (u/v err-val)))` — wraps `err-val` as the `:yang.call/value` of a fresh `:yang.call/thrown` condition.\n    49\t2. **[`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2419-2435`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2419-L2435) (lines `:2431` and `:2433`, invoked at `:2507`)** — `strip-top-outcome` inside [`compile-program`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2398-L2678):\n    50\t   Wraps `(go forms initial-macro-env)` in `(u/let1 top-res ast ...)`; when `(= (data/nth top-res 0) :yin.k/error)` and `(dsg__get top-err :dsg__condition)` is truthy, returns `[:yin.k/error (data/dissoc top-err :dsg__condition)]`.\n    51\t\n    52\t---\n    53\t\n    54\t### 2. Why `test-value-invocation-refusals-are-terminating` (`test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2598`) and the R3 Tests Failed with `:dsg__condition true`\n    55\t\n    56\tIn [`test-value-invocation-refusals-are-terminating`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/clojure_test.clj#L2594-L2637) (`clojure_test.clj:2598`) and the R3 tests (`clojure_test.clj:1486, 1501, 1510, 2735, 2765`), **every failure occurs on `:semantic` while `:walker` passes**:\n    57\t\n    58\t1. In [`test-value-invocation-refusals-are-terminating`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/clojure_test.clj#L2598), `(let [five 5 v [five]] (five 1))` compiles `(five 1)` via `value-call` ([`clojure.cljc:1607-1637`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1607-L1637)) into `(yang.call/invoke invoke-value [five 1] {:yang.call/raise ...})`.\n    59\t2. `invoke-value` ([`clojure.cljc:2794-2795`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2794-L2795)) throws `{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/not-callable, :yang.call/data {:yang.call/callee 5}, :dsg__condition true}`.\n    60\t3. `invoke-value`'s [`call-entry`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1312-L1315) strips `:dsg__condition` and passes `{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/not-callable, :yang.call/data {:yang.call/callee 5}}` to `value-call`'s `:yang.call/raise` handler ([`clojure.cljc:1617-1622`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1617-L1622)).\n    61\t4. `value-call`'s `:yang.call/raise` handler invokes [`condition->outcome`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1543-L1544), which **re-stamps `:dsg__condition true`** and escapes via `dsg__raise_escape` with `[:yin.k/error {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/not-callable, :yang.call/data {:yang.call/callee 5}, :dsg__condition true}]`.\n    62\t5. Because `(let [five 5 v [five]] (five 1))` is a top-level program expression (not inside a `defn`'s `call-entry`), only `strip-top-outcome` ([`clojure.cljc:2419-2435`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2419-L2435), threaded at [`:2507`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2507)) sits between that outcome and the caller.\n    63\t6. **Why `:semantic` skips `strip-top-outcome` completely**:\n    64\t   - Inside [`compile-program`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2446), `go` is initialized with `tail? = true` (`:2446`). As `go` lowers top-level forms ([`:2458-2504`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2458-L2504)), it explicitly attaches `:tail? true` to every top-level sequencing application (`:2479`, `:2492`, `:2504`) and passes `tail? = true` into the final form (`:2495` -> [`compile-guest-call:1656`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1656)).\n    65\t   - Next, [`strip-top-outcome`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2507) wraps `(go forms initial-macro-env)` as the **operand** (`ast`) of `(u/let1 top-res ast ...)` (`((fn [top-res] ...) ast)`).\n    66\t   - At [`clojure.cljc:2674`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2674), `compile-program` runs `(u/mark-tails ...)` ([`yang.tails/mark-tails`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/tails.cljc#L36-L70)), which walks application operands with `tail? = false` ([`tails.cljc:47`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/tails.cljc#L47)) **without stripping pre-existing `:tail? true` marks** (violating the invariant documented at [`src/cljc/yang/tails.cljc:1-6`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/tails.cljc#L1-L6): *\"a stage that wraps a body must strip every mark and recompute them over the whole derived tree, or a wrapped body's old tail calls would skip the wrapper\"*).\n    67\t   - On `:walker` (which ignores `:tail?`), `ast` returns normally into `strip-top-outcome`, which strips `:dsg__condition`. On `:semantic` ([`yin.vm.semantic.linearize:116`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic/linearize.cljc#L116)), the stale `:tail? true` on `ast` emits a tail `:call` (`tail? = true`) that returns directly out of the enclosing frame, **bypassing `strip-top-outcome` completely**.\n    68\t\n    69\t---\n    70\t\n    71\t### 3. Why **(B) Load-bearing** Holds (Why Simply Deleting `:dsg__condition` Fails R3 + R5)\n    72\t\n    73\tIn `yang.clojure`, both:\n    74\t- a caught-and-rethrown protocol condition — e.g., `(defn receive [f] (try (f) (catch Exception e (throw e))))` or across separate activations `(defn retain [f] (try (f) (catch Exception e (cell/set! saved e) e)))` followed by `(defn later [] (throw (cell/get saved)))` ([`test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj:77-79`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj#L77-L79), probed at [`test/yang/call/exception_probes.clj:112-135`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/call/exception_probes.clj#L112-L135)) — and\n    75\t- a guest-thrown condition-shaped data map — e.g., `(defn raise_value [e] (throw e))` with `{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/not-callable, :yang.call/data {:nested [:ordinary :data]}}` ([`test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj:80`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj#L80), probed by `:clojure/shape-throw` at [`test/yang/call/exception_probes.clj:101-110`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/call/exception_probes.clj#L101-L110) and `test-exception-r3-thrown-shape-stays-data` at [`test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1521-1560`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/clojure_test.clj#L1521-L1560))\n    76\t\n    77\tenter the outcome channel through the **exact same `(throw x)` lowering** at [`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1125`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1125) (`[error-tag v]`) and arrive at [`call-entry`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1309-L1317).\n    78\t\n    79\t- If [`call-entry`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1309-L1317) wraps every `err-val` in `(yang.call/thrown err-val)` (as `master` @ `8cd9fc92` does), rethrowing a caught `:yang.call/not-callable` condition double-wraps it into `:yang.call/thrown`, violating **Ruling v2 §R3**.\n    80\t- If [`call-entry`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1309-L1317) inspects only structural keys (`:yang.call/reason`), guest `(throw {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/not-callable, :yang.call/data ...})` is forwarded as a raw `:yang.call/not-callable` refusal instead of `:yang.call/thrown`, violating **Ruling v2 §R2 & §R5**.\n    81\t- Furthermore, because [`check-program-symbols!`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2370-L2394) rejects reserved-prefix **symbols** (`symbol?` at `:2377`, `:2386`) and **not keywords**, guest source can legally write `{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/not-callable, :dsg__condition true}`. Checking `(dsg__get err-val :dsg__condition)` for literal `true` allows guest code to forge a retained condition, violating **Ruling v2 §R5** (*\"forged or source-writable attributes on a wrapper-looking value never authenticate a retained condition\"*).\n    82\t\n    83\t---\n    84\t\n    85\t### 4. Minimal Fix Instruction for the Implementation Round (R3 vs R4 Ownership)\n    86\t\n    87\t#### What R3 Owns and Must Change Now (in [`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc))\n    88\t\n    89\t1. **Fix the stale `:tail? true` mark in [`compile-program`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2446-L2674) so `strip-top-outcome` is never tail-skipped on `:semantic`**:\n    90\t   - At [`src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2674`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2674), replace `u/mark-tails` with [`yang.tails/remark-tails`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/tails.cljc#L72-L77) (or pass `(if (outcomes? env) false tail?)` to `go` at [`:2446`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2446) in addition to `remark-tails`), so pre-existing `:tail? true` marks inside `(go forms initial-macro-env)` are stripped and recomputed after `strip-top-outcome` wraps the body at [`:2507`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2507).\n    91\t2. **Authenticate `:dsg__condition` with the compiler-held closure token `dsg__call_token` instead of literal `true` (satisfying R5)**:\n    92\t   - `dsg__call_token` ([`clojure.cljc:2632`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2632)) is already an unforgeable per-unit closure value (`(fn [x] x)`) in scope of `call-entry`, `compile-guest-call`, `dsg__intra_call`, and `strip-top-outcome`, and its symbol name `dsg__call_token` is blocked in user forms by [`check-program-symbols!`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2370-L2394).\n    93\t   - At writer sites **#1–#6** ([`clojure.cljc:1544, 1548, 1567, 1586, 1636, 2617`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1543-L1636)), stamp `:dsg__condition` with `(u/v 'dsg__call_token)` (and `dsg__call_token` in quoted `dsg__intra_call` at `:2617`) instead of `true`.\n    94\t   - At writer site **#7** (`invoke-value` in `library`, [`clojure.cljc:2794-2795`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2794-L2795)), remove `(throw (assoc ... :dsg__condition true))` and instead trigger a genuine `not-callable` refusal that stamps `dsg__call_token` via compiler-generated code (or invoke `(f)` / a non-callable refusal helper lowered by the compiler), because `library` forms pass through `check-program-symbols!` when spliced in `run-library` tests.\n    95\t   - At the two reader/stripper sites ([`call-entry` at `clojure.cljc:1312-1315`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L1312-L1315) and [`strip-top-outcome` at `clojure.cljc:2430-2433`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc#L2430-L2433)), replace the truthiness check `(dsg__get err-val :dsg__condition)` with token equality `(dsg__equal (dsg__get err-val :dsg__condition) dsg__call_token)`.\n    96\t   - Thus, the external wire condition map leaving `call-entry` (`raise`) or leaving the top-level program (`strip-top-outcome`) is strictly `{:yang.call/reason <kw>, :yang.call/data <map>}` (or `{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown, :yang.call/value <val>}`) with `:dsg__condition` stripped, while inside `try`/`catch` the retained condition carries `:dsg__condition dsg__call_token` so an unchanged `(throw e)` (immediate or delayed via `retain`/`later`) is recognized by `call-entry`, stripped of `:dsg__condition`, and forwarded unchanged.\n    97\t\n    98\t#### What R3 vs R4 Owns\n    99\t\n   100\t- **R3 (Clojure `call-entry` / `compile-guest-call` / `dsg__intra_call` / `strip-top-outcome`)**: Owns the Clojure-side lowering of `yang.call/invoke` outcomes into `[:yin.k/error ...]`, the unforgeable `dsg__call_token` provenance tag on retained conditions, the stripping of `:dsg__condition` at `call-entry` and `strip-top-outcome`, and the `remark-tails` fix in `compile-program`.\n   101\t- **R4 (`test/yang/call/exception_contract.clj`, `test/yang/call/exception_probes.clj`, `test/yang/call/conformance.clj`)**: Owns the cross-language 6-family probe matrix (`:thrown-user-value`, `:thrown-condition-shaped-value`, `:call-refusal-not-callable`, `:call-refusal-invalid-invocation`, `:call-refusal-unsupported-binding`, `:caught-and-rethrown`), including `:clojure/shape-throw` ([`exception_probes.clj:101-110`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/call/exception_probes.clj#L101-L110)) and `:clojure/rethrow-unchanged` (`receive` and `retain`+`later` at [`exception_probes.clj:112-135`](file:///Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/test/yang/call/exception_probes.clj#L112-L135)), asserting that external conditions never leak `:dsg__condition` and that guest-thrown condition-shaped maps never forge a retained condition.\n     1\tCreated-GMT: 2026-11-10 17:33 GMT\n     2\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-11 00:33 +0700\n     3\tCoding-Agent: codex\n     4\tSession-ID: pending (provider-generated)\n     5\t# Task: architect adjudication #2 — the wire shape of a value-path refusal (ruling site #7)\n     6\tRole: Architect (Lead System Architect lane, per the owner's routing ruling)\n     7\tImplementers:\n     8\t- Model: codex (astra) | Assigned: 2026-11-11 00:33 +0700 | Status: active | Rationale: owner directive — codex is available again and takes architect work; this is the semantics call the prior ruling under-specified.\n     9\t\n    10\tThis is a NARROW adjudication continuing\n    11\t`collab/exception-track/1794320926000-architect-dsg-condition-r4-coupling.gemini-4-argon-high.findings.md`\n    12\t(the argon ruling — read section 4 first). Answer ONE question, then give the fix\n    13\tinstruction. Do not re-adjudicate what argon already ruled.\n    14\t\n    15\tWorktree: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch`, branch `exception-r3-impl`\n    16\t@ `98519957` + uncommitted follow-up work (two more fix attempts sit in the tree;\n    17\tread the diff, not just the commit).\n    18\t\n    19\t## The state (verified by the orchestrator, not claimed by a delegate)\n    20\t\n    21\tArgon's ruling: `:dsg__condition` is load-bearing (retained-condition provenance);\n    22\tstamp `dsg__call_token`, read by token equality, `remark-tails` in compile-program.\n    23\tSites #1–#6 and the two readers are FIXED and verified (kit rows\n    24\t`:clojure/protocol-*` green; `:clojure/shape-*` green). One site remains:\n    25\t\n    26\t**Site #7 — `invoke-value`'s `:else`** (clojure.cljc ~2799, the guest collection\n    27\tlibrary's host invoker): a scalar callee, a raw unpublished closure, or a forged\n    28\tshape reaches the library value path and must refuse `not-callable`.\n    29\t\n    30\tArgon's instruction: \"trigger a genuine not-callable refusal that stamps\n    31\t`dsg__call_token` via compiler-generated code (or invoke `(f)` / a non-callable\n    32\trefusal helper lowered by the compiler)\". TWO implementations were attempted; both\n    33\tfail differently, and the failures prove the instruction under-specified the site:\n    34\t\n    35\t- **Attempt A** (`:else (f)` — guest apply): re-enters the call-site dispatcher →\n    36\t  infinite recursion → `Guest instruction budget exhausted` (6 errors). Killed.\n    37\t- **Attempt B** (`:else (throw (yang.call/condition :not-callable {...}))` — bare\n    38\t  host throw): the condition map carries NO token (a host exception cannot carry a\n    39\t  guest closure value). The thrown value crosses back into the guest and the\n    40\t  nearest guest boundary — following §8.10.7, since the map lacks the token —\n    41\t  wraps it: the observable result is\n    42\t  `{:reason :yang.call/thrown, :value {reason :not-callable, data {...}}}`\n    43\t  instead of the raw refusal `{reason :not-callable, data {...}}` the five\n    44\t  pre-existing tests pin (`test-value-invocation-refusals-are-terminating` :2599/\n    45\t  :2619/:2635 ×2 lanes, `test-intra-unit-call-lowering-guards-and-budget` :2736/\n    46\t  :2766 ×2 lanes). Verified: 10 failures / 0 errors, all this one signature.\n    47\t\n    48\t## THE QUESTION\n    49\t\n    50\t**On the library value path (entry-absent callee refused by `invoke-value`'s\n    51\t`:else`), what is the correct observable wire outcome?**\n    52\t\n    53\t**(A) The raw refusal `{reason :not-callable, data {...}}` is the contract** — the\n    54\tvalue path never had a raise exit, refusal-shape is part of the path's own\n    55\tcontract, and the compiler must deliver the token to the library some other way\n    56\t(e.g. the library's `yang.call/condition` helper is invoked through a\n    57\tcompiler-provided adapter that stamps the token host-side; or the refusal is\n    58\tlowered INTO the guest program rather than thrown from the host library). Under\n    59\t(A): name the exact mechanism and the fix.\n    60\t\n    61\t**(B) The `:thrown`-wrapped shape is the contract** — the value path is a host\n    62\tboundary, a thrown condition-map without token is by construction a thrown VALUE,\n    63\tand §8.10.7's wrap is correct; the five pre-existing tests' expectations are the\n    64\tstale artifact and get updated to the wrapped shape (keeping their termination\n    65\tassertions intact). Under (B): say explicitly why this does not violate R5\n    66\t(the wrap is produced by the compiler's own boundary, not by source-writable\n    67\tattributes) and why it does not break the R3 contract (a refusal is not a\n    68\t\"retained condition forwarded unchanged\" — it is a new condition; R3 governs\n    69\tforwarding, not first-construction).\n    70\t\n    71\tWeigh especially: consistency with the already-green `:clojure/protocol-*` rows\n    72\t(those refusals DO arrive as raw conditions — but they travel through\n    73\t`yang.call/invoke`'s eligibility `yang.call/transfer` with a real raise exit,\n    74\twhich the value path lacks); the §8.10.7 rule; R5; and which choice makes R4's\n    75\ttoken-channel story cleanest.\n    76\t\n    77\t## Deliverable\n    78\t\n    79\tOne ruling: (A) or (B), with the reasoning above made explicit, plus the minimal\n    80\tfix instruction (sites, mechanism, and what the five tests' expectations become).\n    81\tDo NOT implement. If you find a third option better than both, rule it and say why.\n    82\t\n    83\tBegin the final response exactly with:\n    84\tCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\n    85\tCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n    1585 collab/exception-track/1794332020000-architect-value-path-refusal-shape.codex-astra.stdout.log\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
