998 1.0000005382899513\\\\\\\")\\\\n   434\\\\t                       \\\\\\\"TypeError\\\\\\\"\\\\n   435\\\\t                       \\\\\\\"TypeError\\\\\\\"\\\\n   436\\\\t                       \\\\\\\"not a context manager\\\\\\\")\\\\n   437\\\\t               (lines \\\\\\\"for i in range(2):\\\\\\\"\\\\n   438\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    try:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   439\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"        raise ValueError('x')\\\\\\\"\\\\n   440\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    finally:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   441\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"        break\\\\\\\"\\\\n   442\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"print('after break')\\\\\\\"\\\\n   443\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"n = 0\\\\\\\"\\\\n   444\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"for i in range(3):\\\\\\\"\\\\n   445\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    try:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   446\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"        raise KeyError('k')\\\\\\\"\\\\n   447\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    finally:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   448\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"        n += 1\\\\\\\"\\\\n   449\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"        continue\\\\\\\"\\\\n   450\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"print(n)\\\\\\\"\\\\n   451\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"class NotExc:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   452\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    pass\\\\\\\"\\\\n   453\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"for v in [1, 'x', NotExc, NotExc()]:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   454\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    try:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   455\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"        raise v\\\\\\\"\\\\n   456\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    except TypeError as e:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   457\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"        print('TypeError', e.args[0])\\\\\\\"\\\\n   458\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"try:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   459\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    raise KeyError('k')\\\\\\\"\\\\n   460\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"except KeyError as err:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   461\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    pass\\\\\\\"\\\\n   462\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"try:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   463\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    print(err)\\\\\\\"\\\\n   464\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"except NameError:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   465\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    print('err unbound')\\\\\\\"\\\\n   466\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"def f():\\\\\\\"\\\\n   467\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"    try:\\\\\\\"\\\\n   468\\\\t                      \\\\\\\"        raise KeyError('k')\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_28\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"All four frontends have a concrete divergence from the full contract. Clojure and Python produce the required condition for fresh escaping exceptions at their public entries; JavaScript and PHP lack that hookup. Foreign reception and unchanged protocol-error rethrow also differ. The local tests pin substantial exception behavior, but I found no real-source language pair exercising §8.10.7 propagation.\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_29\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"agent_message\\\",\\\"text\\\":\\\"The “same continuation” claim is **false in this snapshot**. Clojure and Python connect fresh escaping exceptions to the supplied raise exit; JavaScript and PHP do not. Foreign reception and protocol-error rethrow also diverge.\\\\n\\\\nRead-only audit of `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12`, HEAD `e9a0b430`. No files edited; no tests run. Behavioral consequences below are static deductions from the code.\\\\n\\\\n**yang.clojure — DIVERGENT.**\\\\n\\\\n- **Lowering:** `throw` becomes `[:yin.k/error payload]`. `try` examines that outcome and binds its payload to the handler. It accepts exactly one `Exception`/`ExceptionInfo` catch, performs no runtime class discrimination, and rejects `finally`. See [clojure.cljc:904](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:904).\\\\n- **Raise-exit hookup:** the public entry reads `:yang.call/raise` at line 1070 and invokes it with `(yang.call/thrown payload)` at [clojure.cljc:1088](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1088). Fresh public escapes therefore have the specified condition shape.\\\\n- **Private path:** intra-unit calls pass `[compiler-token args]` and receive outcomes directly; authenticated outcome-token calls also bypass public value/raise conversion. See [clojure.cljc:1110](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1110), [clojure.cljc:1486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1486). Thus its internal exception lowering is an ordinary result channel, unlike the other frontends’ handler continuations.\\\\n- **Foreign reception:** `compile-guest-call` supplies a capture-backed raise closure. A delivered `:yang.call/thrown` becomes an error outcome containing the **raw payload**; another reason becomes an error outcome containing the **whole condition as data**. See [clojure.cljc:1297](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1297). Source catches receive that data. Rethrowing a protocol condition subsequently reaches the public entry’s unconditional `yang.call/thrown`, changing its reason rather than forwarding the original condition.\\\\n- **Root:** `clj/run-main` unwraps successful outcomes and leaves error outcomes as reporting values; it supplies no invocation raise exit. See [clojure/prelude.cljc:87](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:87).\\\\n\\\\n**yang.typescript / JavaScript — DIVERGENT.**\\\\n\\\\n- **Lowering:** `throw` calls `js/throw`; `try/catch` calls `js/try`; an enclosing `finally` calls `js/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:2165](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:2165), line 2214.\\\\n- **Local continuation:** `js/try` captures a guest continuation and installs it in `js.rt/ctx`; `js/throw` walks that runtime’s handler/finally stack. These are guest continuations, not host-native `try/catch`. See [prelude.cljc:1704](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1704).\\\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: absent for escaping source exceptions.** The published function entry directly applies `:code`; it neither reads `:yang.call/raise` nor installs a per-activation exception bridge. See [prelude.cljc:1581](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1581). An escaping throw therefore follows existing JS handlers rather than the foreign caller’s supplied exit. With no JS handler, it reaches the `:js/no-handler` expression.\\\\n- **Foreign reception:** `js/call` really does deliver `:yang.call/value` raw through `js/throw`. However, protocol failures become newly constructed `TypeError`s containing only selected text; the original condition is discarded. See [prelude.cljc:1627](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1627). Unchanged protocol-error rethrow cannot recover it.\\\\n- **Root:** `js/run-main` installs a JS root handler and snapshots uncaught values; it does not repair the missing callee bridge. See [prelude.cljc:1850](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1850).\\\\n\\\\n**yang.php — DIVERGENT.**\\\\n\\\\n- **Lowering:** source throws call `php/throw-value`; catches dispatch through local `php/instance-of`; unmatched catches call `php/raise`; `finally` becomes `php/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:1889](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:1889), line 3600. `throw-value` requires a local Throwable and then raises it. See [prelude.cljc:4084](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4084).\\\\n- **Local continuation:** `php/try` captures a guest handler continuation; `php/raise` walks PHP’s handler/finally stack. With an empty stack, `php/raise` **returns the error normally**. See [prelude.cljc:190](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:190).\\\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: absent for source/binding exceptions.** The function entry performs named-argument placement and applies `:code` without an exception bridge. Its supplied raise exit is used only for unsupported binding profiles. See [prelude.cljc:2264](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2264), line 2281. The Closure entry likewise ignores the supplied exit and delegates through `php/call`: [prelude.cljc:4217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4217).\\\\n- **Foreign reception:** `php/call`, `call-with-binding-profile`, and `call-named` convert not-callable to a fresh local `Error`; otherwise they call **`php/raise condition`**, without extracting `:yang.call/value`. See [prelude.cljc:2384](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2384), line 4314. Even an original PHP Throwable arriving inside a valid thrown condition is therefore delivered as its envelope, not restored.\\\\n- **`ForeignException`: nowhere.** No wrapper, local hierarchy, or identity-owned retained-condition association exists. `catch (Throwable)` receives no valid local wrapper; explicitly throwing the envelope cannot preserve the original condition.\\\\n- **Root:** `php/run-main` catches through PHP’s local stack and snapshots the result. See [prelude.cljc:5070](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5070).\\\\n\\\\n**yang.python — DIVERGENT.**\\\\n\\\\n- **Lowering:** `raise X` calls `py/as-exception` then `py/raise`; bare rethrow uses the captured exception directly. Typed clauses use `py/exc-matches`, bare `except` selects its handler unconditionally, and `finally` becomes `py/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:1664](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:1664), lines 1699 and 1759.\\\\n- **Local continuation:** `py/try` captures and installs a guest handler, restoring saved context before calling its handler; `py/raise` runs intervening finally frames. See [prelude.cljc:294](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:294).\\\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: present.** `py/function-entry` reads the supplied exit at line 893 and places **both argument binding and body execution** inside `py/try`. Its handler invokes that exit with the required `:yang.call/thrown`/`:yang.call/value` condition. See [prelude.cljc:886](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:886), line 912. Fresh escaping Python exceptions and binding failures follow the intended boundary path.\\\\n- **Foreign reception:** `py/call-kw` delivers every thrown payload directly through `py/raise`, without validating whether it is a local exception or wrapping foreign data. Protocol failures become a generic `TypeError`, losing the condition. See [prelude.cljc:1034](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1034).\\\\n- **`ForeignException`: nowhere.** Bare `except` can receive raw foreign data, but typed catches use `py/type-of` and the local hierarchy; foreign data has no generated local exception class. Explicit `raise e` applies `py/as-exception` and can replace that foreign payload with a `TypeError`. See [prelude.cljc:352](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:352), lines 754 and 802. There is no wrapper-lifetime condition association to recover in another activation.\\\\n- **Root:** `py/run-main` catches and snapshots through Python’s local stack. See [prelude.cljc:3322](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:3322).\\\\n\\\\n**Local coverage inventory — assertions present, not rerun.**\\\\n\\\\n| Frontend | What its own tests pin |\\\\n|---|---|\\\\n| Clojure | Throw/error outcomes: [clojure_test.clj:1162](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1162). Broad admitted catch, nested replacement throw, operand escape, and rejection of `finally`/`Throwable`: [line 2452](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2452). Named binding failure emits `:yang.call/thrown`: line 1429. No unchanged-rethrow identity assertion found in these cases. |\\\\n| JavaScript | Object and primitive throws, catch-all, finally on normal/return exits, string rethrow, uncaught exceptions: [e2e_test.clj:254](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/e2e_test.clj:254). Finally ordering/escape cleanup: [prelude_parity_test.cljc:677](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:677), line 785. Typed catch syntax is inapplicable. |\\\\n| PHP | Throwable hierarchy, typed/multi-type catches, `Throwable` catch-all, unmatched propagation, rethrow, finally/return override and exception replacement: [exceptions_test.clj:19](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/php/antlr/exceptions_test.clj:19), lines 33, 48 and 63. The test labelled “rethrow keeps the same object” checks its message, **not object identity**. |\\\\n| Python | Typed catches across a local callee, bare rethrow, uncaught errors: [e2e_test.clj:534](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_test.clj:534), lines 554 and 609. Bare catch-all lowering: [lower_test.clj:197](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/lower_test.clj:197). Finally on every exit, nested cleanup, return override and replacement exception: [e2e_c1_test.clj:30](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c1_test.clj:30), lines 62 and 91. Binder condition shape: [call_entry_test.cljc:76](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/call_entry_test.cljc:76). |\\\\n\\\\n**Cross-language evidence: the “zero exception legs” report is confirmed for the checked-in conformance tests.**\\\\n\\\\nThe shared kit composes donor exports and runs caller source; it contains no exception scenario itself: [conformance.clj:245](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/call/conformance.clj:245). Its real-source consumers in [cross_lang_test.clj:26](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj:26), Python’s [source_call_test.clj:9](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/source_call_test.clj:9), and TS/PHP’s [source_call_test.cljc:98](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/source_call_test.cljc:98) exercise successful calls, callbacks, identity and opaque values—not escaping exceptions.\\\\n\\\\nThere **is** a JavaScript reception fixture asserting payload identity after a hand-built entry invokes the supplied raise exit: [prelude_parity_test.cljc:768](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:768). That is useful helper evidence, but no second frontend compiles the throw. Likewise, shared helper tests construct conditions directly: [call_test.cljc:157](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/core/call_test.cljc:157).\\\\n\\\\nConsequently, no real-source language pair currently establishes the §8.10.7 propagation contract, including binding-failure propagation, foreign typed/catch-all reception, unchanged/protocol rethrow, finally replacement, A→B→A cleanup and identity, or saved `ForeignException` rethrow from another activation.\\\\n\\\\n**Ranked divergences:**\\\\n\\\\n1. **Missing callee exception bridges in JavaScript and PHP.** Their entries bypass the supplied raise exit for source exceptions; PHP binding failures also occur outside such a bridge. Evidence: JS prelude line 1581; PHP prelude lines 2264 and 4217.\\\\n2. **Missing foreign-exception reception in Python and PHP.** Python raises raw foreign payloads; PHP raises the entire condition envelope. Neither implements `ForeignException` or its lifetime-owned condition association. Evidence: Python prelude line 1034; PHP prelude line 2384.\\\\n3. **Original protocol conditions do not survive unchanged rethrow.** JS/Python discard them while constructing local errors; Clojure retains them as catch data but wraps a rethrow in a new thrown condition; PHP supplies an envelope without a valid local wrapper. Evidence: JS line 1634; Python line 1046; Clojure lines 1306 and 1088; PHP lines 2391 and 4085.\\\\n4. **Clojure has a distinct internal failure channel and restricted catch/finally lowering.** Error vectors and authenticated private calls carry failures as ordinary results before public conversion. Evidence: compiler lines 904, 1110 and 1486.\\\\n\\\\nI found no synthesized engine-effect exception value, guessed foreign class-name matching, or source-map detector that forwards data merely because it looks like a condition. Dispatch on the reason of an actually delivered boundary condition is distinct from that forbidden shape inference.\\\\n\\\\n**Overall verdict: DIVERGENT.**\\\"}}\\n{\\\"type\\\":\\\"turn.completed\\\",\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"input_tokens\\\":1190399,\\\"cached_input_tokens\\\":1069312,\\\"cache_write_input_tokens\\\":0,\\\"output_tokens\\\":10086,\\\"reasoning_output_tokens\\\":2837}}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n1151:collab/exception-track/1794930000000-architect-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:21:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_9\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n '8\\\\\\\\.10\\\\\\\\.[0-9]+|ForeignException|exception|dart|four VM|four vm' docs/design/yang.antlr.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"41:master since: captured continuations are invocable on all four VMs\\n1146:  `cell/authentic?` query (8.10.12) compiled call routing asks before\\n1223:collections, language exceptions, argument binding, and other\\n1288:- Language exceptions and cleanup behavior.\\n1335:  injected exceptions: the generator is a mutable identity whose saved\\n1396:| `RecursionError`, `setrecursionlimit`        | 1                      | Continuations are heap data on all four VMs, so nothing overflows; a depth   |\\n1570:tag: one would change the grammar on four VMs and the codec for a fact\\n1676:|                     |                 | `:exception` trace event.            |\\n1686:in one cell, generalizing `py.rt/handlers`. An exception can unwind many\\n1772:  raise `NotImplementedError`. Not emitted: `exception` and C-call\\n1797:Slice 1 acceptance runs on all four VMs:\\n1885:  which already pass the exception to any non-`:finally` frame's\\n1891:  `:loop` and `:ret` escapes; only exceptions and yields cross, both\\n1902:exception per item.\\n1925:  exception, `GeneratorExit`, or `return` unwinds them through the\\n1961:exceptions propagate, and optional `send`, `throw`, and `close` are\\n2039:  suspension during exception unwinding.\\n2050:  `StopIteration`, and exceptions from loop bodies.\\n2103:serialized bytes, task-cell allocation counts, guest exceptions, or\\n2172:change. Python dispatch, sign rules, and exceptions stay in the\\n2182:| Guest exception construction         | Exact add, subtract, multiply,        |\\n2461:| S3a    | Limit reasons as guest exceptions: `integer` version 2 returns      |\\n2492:- All four VMs on JVM, Node, and Dart VM, twelve lanes; Dart compiled to\\n2515:on the four VMs on every host; `c3_gate_parser_test` binds each packet\\n2822:running the body, and runs the body; on an exception it removes the entry\\n2845:exceptions keep canonical builtin class references even when guest code\\n2847:global shadow and delete, and cross-module exception identity.\\n2920:`py/import-refused` turns delivery refusals into exceptions at the\\n2928:refusal retains the complete structured map on the exception's\\n3074:  consumption, runtime initialization, and exception parity, not merely\\n3166:store-context transitions on all four VMs. Until B lands, a forged\\n3270:  revisions keeps one program root; an exception raised in one unit is\\n3306:  exception from a function in `m` on every VM; one image as main and as\\n3525:#### 8.10.1 Decision and scope\\n3555:#### 8.10.2 Callable representation and identity\\n3580:(`cell/authentic?`, section 8.10.12); an unauthenticated shape projects\\n3607:VM application defect, not a source exception caught by the shared helper.\\n3651:#### 8.10.3 Invocation record\\n3678:  guest exit, accepting the condition in section 8.10.7. It closes over\\n3679:  the caller's continuation and exception machinery, never a host\\n3690:section 8.10.6. The callee entry compares the supplied profile address\\n3717:that is an operational wiring failure; do not invent a source exception\\n3723:#### 8.10.4 Source semantics at caller and callee\\n3749:The callee entry installs its exception boundary **before parameter\\n3777:#### 8.10.5 Values and projections\\n3813:#### 8.10.6 References, higher-order calls, and ownership\\n3838:#### 8.10.7 Normal return and escaping conditions\\n3844:An escaping source exception invokes the supplied raise exit with:\\n3851:This is the argument to an exceptional continuation transfer, not an\\n3861:them to a documented local exception form retaining the complete\\n3863:callee exception under `:yang.call/thrown`. These reasons do not extend\\n3866:The receiving caller interprets an escaping exception as follows:\\n3868:1. A payload valid as an exception in its own runtime retains that\\n3872:3. A runtime requiring exception objects, such as Python or PHP,\\n3874:   `ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and original\\n3878:`ForeignException` is a proposed prelude-owned type, not a VM type.\\n3880:wrappers. Raising a new exception creates a new condition. Crossing back\\n3881:into a runtime recognizing the original exception restores its original\\n3897:`:yang.call/thrown`; its local exception for a protocol failure must still\\n3899:exception forwards that complete condition, including protocol reasons;\\n3906:source exceptions. Existing explicit stream/module refusals keep their\\n3909:#### 8.10.8 Cleanup, re-entry, and suspension\\n3911:Each activation establishes its own exception bridge and saves its\\n3916:or exception does so before common propagation under its own rules.\\n3923:inner exception as specified in section 8.10.7.\\n3943:#### 8.10.9 Publication, visibility, and reproducibility\\n3974:exceptions, effects, suspension, and identity. Correctness must not\\n3977:#### 8.10.10 Current code and migration\\n3995:   exception boundaries under the entry. Keep existing source `:call`\\n4016:#### 8.10.11 Acceptance gates\\n4026:  required/extra arguments, keyword errors, receivers, exceptions, and\\n4060:  propagation. Assert original exception identity and cleanup order,\\n4061:  not only rendered text. Store/return a generated `ForeignException`,\\n4072:  Node, and Dart over the four VMs wherever existing contracts admit\\n4081:#### 8.10.12 Cell-reference call routing and authenticity\\n4136:- **Entry-key exception.** \\\"Ordinary map application always remains\\n4141:  declared shape-routed exception and is unchanged by this ruling.\\n4146:  (8.10.11) bind every specialization.\\n4364:coercions, collections, and language exceptions.\\n4467:6. Retain no exception, recognizer, token, or host stack object in\\n4566:surfaces as a deterministic guest exception (C3 ruling 11).\\n4641:  prelude level on all four VMs on every host and from source on the\\n4671:- Java numeric and exception semantics.\\n4774:9. **Semantic preservation:** observable values, mutation, exceptions,\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n1152:collab/exception-track/1794930000000-architect-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:25:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_10\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"python3 -c 'import json; p=\\\\\\\"collab/yang-python/1794900000000-reviewer-exception-uniformity-audit.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\\\\\\\"; xs=[json.loads(l) for l in open(p)]; print([x[\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\"][\\\\\\\"text\\\\\\\"] for x in xs if x.get(\\\\\\\"item\\\\\\\",{}).get(\\\\\\\"type\\\\\\\")==\\\\\\\"agent_message\\\\\\\"][-1])'\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"python3: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\npython3: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-bDDKo7Bc' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-07 09:36:43.480 xcodebuild[12131:113771985]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-07 09:36:43.711 xcodebuild[12131:113771924] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\npython3: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\\npython3: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-pNXYhcmk' (errno=Operation not permitted)\\n2026-11-07 09:36:44.534 xcodebuild[12147:113772062]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\\n2026-11-07 09:36:44.749 xcodebuild[12147:113772059] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \\\"Input/output error\\\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\\nThe “same continuation” claim is **false in this snapshot**. Clojure and Python connect fresh escaping exceptions to the supplied raise exit; JavaScript and PHP do not. Foreign reception and protocol-error rethrow also diverge.\\n\\nRead-only audit of `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12`, HEAD `e9a0b430`. No files edited; no tests run. Behavioral consequences below are static deductions from the code.\\n\\n**yang.clojure — DIVERGENT.**\\n\\n- **Lowering:** `throw` becomes `[:yin.k/error payload]`. `try` examines that outcome and binds its payload to the handler. It accepts exactly one `Exception`/`ExceptionInfo` catch, performs no runtime class discrimination, and rejects `finally`. See [clojure.cljc:904](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:904).\\n- **Raise-exit hookup:** the public entry reads `:yang.call/raise` at line 1070 and invokes it with `(yang.call/thrown payload)` at [clojure.cljc:1088](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1088). Fresh public escapes therefore have the specified condition shape.\\n- **Private path:** intra-unit calls pass `[compiler-token args]` and receive outcomes directly; authenticated outcome-token calls also bypass public value/raise conversion. See [clojure.cljc:1110](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1110), [clojure.cljc:1486](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1486). Thus its internal exception lowering is an ordinary result channel, unlike the other frontends’ handler continuations.\\n- **Foreign reception:** `compile-guest-call` supplies a capture-backed raise closure. A delivered `:yang.call/thrown` becomes an error outcome containing the **raw payload**; another reason becomes an error outcome containing the **whole condition as data**. See [clojure.cljc:1297](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1297). Source catches receive that data. Rethrowing a protocol condition subsequently reaches the public entry’s unconditional `yang.call/thrown`, changing its reason rather than forwarding the original condition.\\n- **Root:** `clj/run-main` unwraps successful outcomes and leaves error outcomes as reporting values; it supplies no invocation raise exit. See [clojure/prelude.cljc:87](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:87).\\n\\n**yang.typescript / JavaScript — DIVERGENT.**\\n\\n- **Lowering:** `throw` calls `js/throw`; `try/catch` calls `js/try`; an enclosing `finally` calls `js/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:2165](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/lower.cljc:2165), line 2214.\\n- **Local continuation:** `js/try` captures a guest continuation and installs it in `js.rt/ctx`; `js/throw` walks that runtime’s handler/finally stack. These are guest continuations, not host-native `try/catch`. See [prelude.cljc:1704](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1704).\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: absent for escaping source exceptions.** The published function entry directly applies `:code`; it neither reads `:yang.call/raise` nor installs a per-activation exception bridge. See [prelude.cljc:1581](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1581). An escaping throw therefore follows existing JS handlers rather than the foreign caller’s supplied exit. With no JS handler, it reaches the `:js/no-handler` expression.\\n- **Foreign reception:** `js/call` really does deliver `:yang.call/value` raw through `js/throw`. However, protocol failures become newly constructed `TypeError`s containing only selected text; the original condition is discarded. See [prelude.cljc:1627](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1627). Unchanged protocol-error rethrow cannot recover it.\\n- **Root:** `js/run-main` installs a JS root handler and snapshots uncaught values; it does not repair the missing callee bridge. See [prelude.cljc:1850](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:1850).\\n\\n**yang.php — DIVERGENT.**\\n\\n- **Lowering:** source throws call `php/throw-value`; catches dispatch through local `php/instance-of`; unmatched catches call `php/raise`; `finally` becomes `php/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:1889](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/lower.cljc:1889), line 3600. `throw-value` requires a local Throwable and then raises it. See [prelude.cljc:4084](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4084).\\n- **Local continuation:** `php/try` captures a guest handler continuation; `php/raise` walks PHP’s handler/finally stack. With an empty stack, `php/raise` **returns the error normally**. See [prelude.cljc:190](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:190).\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: absent for source/binding exceptions.** The function entry performs named-argument placement and applies `:code` without an exception bridge. Its supplied raise exit is used only for unsupported binding profiles. See [prelude.cljc:2264](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2264), line 2281. The Closure entry likewise ignores the supplied exit and delegates through `php/call`: [prelude.cljc:4217](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:4217).\\n- **Foreign reception:** `php/call`, `call-with-binding-profile`, and `call-named` convert not-callable to a fresh local `Error`; otherwise they call **`php/raise condition`**, without extracting `:yang.call/value`. See [prelude.cljc:2384](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:2384), line 4314. Even an original PHP Throwable arriving inside a valid thrown condition is therefore delivered as its envelope, not restored.\\n- **`ForeignException`: nowhere.** No wrapper, local hierarchy, or identity-owned retained-condition association exists. `catch (Throwable)` receives no valid local wrapper; explicitly throwing the envelope cannot preserve the original condition.\\n- **Root:** `php/run-main` catches through PHP’s local stack and snapshots the result. See [prelude.cljc:5070](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc:5070).\\n\\n**yang.python — DIVERGENT.**\\n\\n- **Lowering:** `raise X` calls `py/as-exception` then `py/raise`; bare rethrow uses the captured exception directly. Typed clauses use `py/exc-matches`, bare `except` selects its handler unconditionally, and `finally` becomes `py/try-finally`. See [lower.cljc:1664](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/lower.cljc:1664), lines 1699 and 1759.\\n- **Local continuation:** `py/try` captures and installs a guest handler, restoring saved context before calling its handler; `py/raise` runs intervening finally frames. See [prelude.cljc:294](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:294).\\n- **Raise-exit hookup: present.** `py/function-entry` reads the supplied exit at line 893 and places **both argument binding and body execution** inside `py/try`. Its handler invokes that exit with the required `:yang.call/thrown`/`:yang.call/value` condition. See [prelude.cljc:886](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:886), line 912. Fresh escaping Python exceptions and binding failures follow the intended boundary path.\\n- **Foreign reception:** `py/call-kw` delivers every thrown payload directly through `py/raise`, without validating whether it is a local exception or wrapping foreign data. Protocol failures become a generic `TypeError`, losing the condition. See [prelude.cljc:1034](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:1034).\\n- **`ForeignException`: nowhere.** Bare `except` can receive raw foreign data, but typed catches use `py/type-of` and the local hierarchy; foreign data has no generated local exception class. Explicit `raise e` applies `py/as-exception` and can replace that foreign payload with a `TypeError`. See [prelude.cljc:352](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:352), lines 754 and 802. There is no wrapper-lifetime condition association to recover in another activation.\\n- **Root:** `py/run-main` catches and snapshots through Python’s local stack. See [prelude.cljc:3322](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/src/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc:3322).\\n\\n**Local coverage inventory — assertions present, not rerun.**\\n\\n| Frontend | What its own tests pin |\\n|---|---|\\n| Clojure | Throw/error outcomes: [clojure_test.clj:1162](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:1162). Broad admitted catch, nested replacement throw, operand escape, and rejection of `finally`/`Throwable`: [line 2452](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2452). Named binding failure emits `:yang.call/thrown`: line 1429. No unchanged-rethrow identity assertion found in these cases. |\\n| JavaScript | Object and primitive throws, catch-all, finally on normal/return exits, string rethrow, uncaught exceptions: [e2e_test.clj:254](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/e2e_test.clj:254). Finally ordering/escape cleanup: [prelude_parity_test.cljc:677](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:677), line 785. Typed catch syntax is inapplicable. |\\n| PHP | Throwable hierarchy, typed/multi-type catches, `Throwable` catch-all, unmatched propagation, rethrow, finally/return override and exception replacement: [exceptions_test.clj:19](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/php/antlr/exceptions_test.clj:19), lines 33, 48 and 63. The test labelled “rethrow keeps the same object” checks its message, **not object identity**. |\\n| Python | Typed catches across a local callee, bare rethrow, uncaught errors: [e2e_test.clj:534](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_test.clj:534), lines 554 and 609. Bare catch-all lowering: [lower_test.clj:197](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/lower_test.clj:197). Finally on every exit, nested cleanup, return override and replacement exception: [e2e_c1_test.clj:30](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c1_test.clj:30), lines 62 and 91. Binder condition shape: [call_entry_test.cljc:76](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/call_entry_test.cljc:76). |\\n\\n**Cross-language evidence: the “zero exception legs” report is confirmed for the checked-in conformance tests.**\\n\\nThe shared kit composes donor exports and runs caller source; it contains no exception scenario itself: [conformance.clj:245](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/call/conformance.clj:245). Its real-source consumers in [cross_lang_test.clj:26](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj:26), Python’s [source_call_test.clj:9](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/python/antlr/source_call_test.clj:9), and TS/PHP’s [source_call_test.cljc:98](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/source_call_test.cljc:98) exercise successful calls, callbacks, identity and opaque values—not escaping exceptions.\\n\\nThere **is** a JavaScript reception fixture asserting payload identity after a hand-built entry invokes the supplied raise exit: [prelude_parity_test.cljc:768](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:768). That is useful helper evidence, but no second frontend compiles the throw. Likewise, shared helper tests construct conditions directly: [call_test.cljc:157](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-f12/test/yang/core/call_test.cljc:157).\\n\\nConsequently, no real-source language pair currently establishes the §8.10.7 propagation contract, including binding-failure propagation, foreign typed/catch-all reception, unchanged/protocol rethrow, finally replacement, A→B→A cleanup and identity, or saved `ForeignException` rethrow from another activation.\\n\\n**Ranked divergences:**\\n\\n1. **Missing callee exception bridges in JavaScript and PHP.** Their entries bypass the supplied raise exit for source exceptions; PHP binding failures also occur outside such a bridge. Evidence: JS prelude line 1581; PHP prelude lines 2264 and 4217.\\n2. **Missing foreign-exception reception in Python and PHP.** Python raises raw foreign payloads; PHP raises the entire condition envelope. Neither implements `ForeignException` or its lifetime-owned condition association. Evidence: Python prelude line 1034; PHP prelude line 2384.\\n3. **Original protocol conditions do not survive unchanged rethrow.** JS/Python discard them while constructing local errors; Clojure retains them as catch data but wraps a rethrow in a new thrown condition; PHP supplies an envelope without a valid local wrapper. Evidence: JS line 1634; Python line 1046; Clojure lines 1306 and 1088; PHP lines 2391 and 4085.\\n4. **Clojure has a distinct internal failure channel and restricted catch/finally lowering.** Error vectors and authenticated private calls carry failures as ordinary results before public conversion. Evidence: compiler lines 904, 1110 and 1486.\\n\\nI found no synthesized engine-effect exception value, guessed foreign class-name matching, or source-map detector that forwards data merely because it looks like a condition. Dispatch on the reason of an actually delivered boundary condition is distinct from that forbidden shape inference.\\n\\n**Overall verdict: DIVERGENT.**\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n1153:collab/exception-track/1794930000000-architect-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:29:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_13\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '3651,3758p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md; sed -n '3838,4047p' docs/design/yang.antlr.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"#### 8.10.3 Invocation record\\n\\nThe invocation is an ephemeral in-machine value. These keys are proposed\\nvocabulary, not existing repository APIs:\\n\\n```clojure\\n{:yang.call/callee     f\\n :yang.call/positional [v0 v1 ...]\\n :yang.call/named      [[\\\"name\\\" value] ...]\\n :yang.call/raise      raise-exit\\n ;; Optional: presence means a receiver was supplied, even when nil.\\n :yang.call/receiver   receiver\\n ;; Optional: pinned argument-binding extension, for example locations.\\n :yang.call/binding-profile \\\"<content-address>\\\"}\\n```\\n\\nThe four required fields mean:\\n\\n- `:yang.call/callee`: the original resolved callable, including its\\n  identity. The invocation helper supplies it; an extracted entry must\\n  not be applied to another object's substituted state.\\n- `:yang.call/positional`: a dense ordered vector of evaluated values.\\n  Its length records presence; no missing-argument sentinel is inserted.\\n- `:yang.call/named`: ordered name/value pairs, not a map. Names are\\n  plain text without caller-side case folding or normalization.\\n  Duplicate names remain visible until the callee's binder decides them.\\n- `:yang.call/raise`: this activation's caller-supplied, non-returning\\n  guest exit, accepting the condition in section 8.10.7. It closes over\\n  the caller's continuation and exception machinery, never a host\\n  callback or hidden global handler slot.\\n\\nAn absent receiver differs from one whose value is `nil`, `:py/None`,\\n`:php/null`, `:js/null`, or `:js/undefined`. Empty argument vectors are\\nrequired rather than represented by absent keys. Presence must not be\\ninferred from truthiness.\\n\\nThe optional binding profile pins additional argument semantics. It\\ndoes not name the caller's language or select a language-pair bridge.\\nAbsent that extension, arguments are by-value runtime values as in\\nsection 8.10.6. The callee entry compares the supplied profile address\\nwith its declared accepted/required binding profiles before acquiring\\nreferences or entering the body. An unsupported required profile or a\\nmissing required extension fails there. The pinned profile defines the\\nshape and validation of any extra invocation fields it requires; values\\nin the base vectors alone do not establish a location protocol.\\n\\nThe PHP reference-argument profile is pinned as\\n`sha256:171656777d777865312132fe18cbc7a057a4721844a4dc450c0800381d2223eb`.\\nPHP call sites include it when their declared target has reference\\nparameters; corresponding positional values may then be PHP reference\\nmarkers for caller locations. The PHP entry rejects unknown profile\\naddresses before parameter binding, and only this profile authorizes its\\nbinder to attach a parameter to a caller marker. The markers alone carry\\nno binding authority.\\n\\nConvention revision is pinned by the existing lowering/runtime profiles.\\nIt is not a new canonical AST slot, a per-function language tag, or a\\nrepeated version field in every invocation. Incompatible revisions need\\nnew profiles and explicit admission, never entry-shape guessing.\\n\\nThe shared helper validates the record's map shape, required presence,\\npositional vector, and ordered pairs with text names. The callee entry\\nvalidates extension fields under its admitted binding profile; the shared\\nhelper knows no extension-specific representation. A valid caller-owned\\nraise exit is established before constructing the record, so structural\\nfailures can be transferred through it. If no valid raise exit exists,\\nthat is an operational wiring failure; do not invent a source exception\\npath, default missing fields to `nil`, or broadly catch VM application\\nerrors. Checking live entry/exit/predicate closure arity is not part of\\nthis validation; constructor fixtures and admission under pinned\\nprofiles establish those obligations.\\n\\n#### 8.10.4 Source semantics at caller and callee\\n\\nThe caller's lowering preserves its source rules for callable resolution,\\nargument/receiver evaluation, and spread or unpacking. Evaluate each\\nexpression exactly once, in the source-defined order. Errors required\\nbefore argument evaluation must still occur before it. The record holds\\nevaluated values; the callee must not reevaluate source expressions.\\n\\nAfter source resolution, calls use the common projection and application.\\nFor a cell-backed callable, the successful path is schematically:\\n\\n```clojure\\n;; UAST notation after source evaluation/resolution and validation.\\n;; The caller has already established `raise-exit`.\\n(let [entry (get (cell/get f) :yang.call/entry)\\n      invocation (assoc evaluated-arguments\\n                        :yang.call/callee f\\n                        :yang.call/raise raise-exit)]\\n  (entry invocation))\\n```\\n\\nThe shared prelude handles projection and validation for cell-backed and\\nimmutable callables. Existing `py/call`, `js/call`, or `php/call` names\\nmay remain as source call-site helpers, but must not reject an admitted\\nforeign callable because its native type tag differs.\\n\\nThe callee entry installs its exception boundary **before parameter\\nbinding**. It owns defaults, arity checks, positional/named/variadic\\nbinding, receiver interpretation, and supported source constraints and\\ncoercions. It then invokes its body using its private lowered signature\\nand captured environment.\\n\\nEntry boilerplate is not an additional source function activation.\\nSource recursion limits, tracing, and safepoint call marks must count the\\noriginal source activation once, not both the entry and its private body\\nas two source calls. Genuine nested source calls remain observable.\\n#### 8.10.7 Normal return and escaping conditions\\n\\nNormal return is the value of ordinary Yin application. There is no\\nextra return continuation, completion packet around each result, or new\\nlanguage-specific return instruction.\\n\\nAn escaping source exception invokes the supplied raise exit with:\\n\\n```clojure\\n{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown\\n :yang.call/value  original-thrown-value}\\n```\\n\\nThis is the argument to an exceptional continuation transfer, not an\\nordinary return. Returning an identical-looking map returns normal data.\\nLikewise, a source program throwing a map with these keys throws that\\nmap as the **payload of a new condition**; its shape is never evidence\\nthat it is already a condition to forward. No engine-effect value is\\nsynthesized.\\n\\nProtocol failures use qualified reasons such as\\n`:yang.call/not-callable`, `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`, and\\n`:yang.call/unsupported-binding`, with plain evidence. The caller maps\\nthem to a documented local exception form retaining the complete\\noriginal condition. Callee argument-binding failures retain the actual\\ncallee exception under `:yang.call/thrown`. These reasons do not extend\\n`dao.stream`'s outcome algebra.\\n\\nThe receiving caller interprets an escaping exception as follows:\\n\\n1. A payload valid as an exception in its own runtime retains that\\n   runtime's ordinary catching and identity behavior.\\n2. A runtime permitting arbitrary thrown values, such as JavaScript,\\n   may deliver the original payload directly.\\n3. A runtime requiring exception objects, such as Python or PHP,\\n   represents an otherwise foreign payload with its own\\n   `ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and original\\n   payload. Catch-all catches this local object. Typed source catches\\n   match its local hierarchy, never guessed foreign class names.\\n\\n`ForeignException` is a proposed prelude-owned type, not a VM type.\\nUnchanged rethrow propagates the original condition without adding\\nwrappers. Raising a new exception creates a new condition. Crossing back\\ninto a runtime recognizing the original exception restores its original\\ntype and identity. Formatting a foreign payload must not implicitly\\ninvoke a foreign method.\\n\\nThe runtime associates its generated wrapper's identity with the retained\\ncondition for the wrapper's entire lifetime, separate from source-writable\\nattributes. The association normally lives in private wrapper-cell\\ncontents, or an equivalent task-owned identity structure with the same\\nlifetime. It survives storing or returning the wrapper after the creating\\ncall/catch activation has ended, and remains recoverable when that same\\nwrapper is rethrown in another activation. Temporary catch state alone is\\ninsufficient. Reachability/reclamation must retain the condition and its\\npayload while the wrapper is live, without an ambient registry.\\n\\nThe runtime must not detect wrappers or unwrap arbitrary maps by their\\nkey names. JavaScript may expose raw payloads for\\n`:yang.call/thrown`; its local exception for a protocol failure must still\\nretain that failure's qualified condition. Rethrowing the same generated\\nexception forwards that complete condition, including protocol reasons;\\nthrowing an unrelated value creates a new `:yang.call/thrown` condition.\\nSaved activation state refers to this identity-owned association; exiting\\nor restoring a catch does not destroy it.\\n\\nVM application/custody defects and host implementation failures remain\\noperational failures. Do not catch them broadly and relabel them as\\nsource exceptions. Existing explicit stream/module refusals keep their\\nsource mappings under the relevant profiles.\\n\\n#### 8.10.8 Cleanup, re-entry, and suspension\\n\\nEach activation establishes its own exception bridge and saves its\\ncallee runtime's dynamic context. Binding, body, and exit run under that\\nbridge. Before invoking the caller's raise exit, let source catches and\\ncleanup complete and restore saved handler/depth/frame state. Normal\\nreturn restores it too. A source `finally` replacing a pending return\\nor exception does so before common propagation under its own rules.\\n\\nIn A-to-B-to-A calls, the innermost activation must not jump to an older\\nA handler past B. Its entry bridge is the boundary: inner A cleanup\\nprecedes B cleanup, which precedes outer A handling. Every activation\\nowns its saved state and raise exit, without a single current-foreign-\\nhandler cell or ambient dispatcher. Unchanged rethrow preserves the\\ninner exception as specified in section 8.10.7.\\n\\nCleanup runs once per actual exit. Resuming a suspended VM transition\\nmust not repeat parameter binding, argument evaluation, defaults,\\nreference acquisition, or completed cleanup. The record, continuations,\\nand explicit runtime state use the existing continuation machinery and\\nits admission restrictions.\\n\\nA generator or asynchronous function returns its source-defined object.\\nSuspension on a stream effect keeps the activation; it is not a new\\ncall. A later generator resume or scheduled job has a new activation and\\nvalid bridge, never the raise exit of a call that already returned.\\nUnrestricted continuation escape across foreign frames is not implicitly\\nsupported: a claiming profile must define and test restoration and\\nreference lifetimes for every crossed frame.\\n\\nInvoking a function-valued argument is internal Yin computation.\\nHost-initiated callbacks remain stream events under\\n`datom.world.abi.md`; this convention adds no host callback facility.\\n\\n#### 8.10.9 Publication, visibility, and reproducibility\\n\\nShared callability creates no common global scope. An import or\\ncomposition explicitly binds exports in the receiving source namespace\\nunder its normal name/module rules. Missing names keep their source\\nerrors. No fallback searches other frontends, the filesystem, a host\\npackage registry, or ambient globals.\\n\\nLinked modules publish code and immutable specifications. Their bodies\\ninstantiate function objects and common entries in the importing task,\\nas section 8.5.6 requires. Install children must not export live function\\ncells. A higher-order result is an ordinary runtime value, not a new\\nlinker publication on each call.\\n\\nLowering/runtime profiles pin the implementation and binding/value\\nextensions. Existing dependency/effect/custody checks remain. Type facts\\nstay beside code under `yin.vm.typecheck`, not in lambda language tags.\\nDo not duplicate existing lambda parameters in another persisted\\nauthority over the private body signature.\\n\\nBefore admitting a compiled composition, its profile/dependency\\ndeclarations must establish one compatible convention revision for the\\nentries it exposes. An incompatible revision needs an explicitly pinned\\nprojection or is refused before execution. Neither a common slot name nor\\nan opaque runtime closure proves revision compatibility. No runtime\\nlanguage registry or inspection of captured environments supplies it.\\n\\nChanged entry code changes code identities through the normal encoder.\\nRetain source, lowered UAST, pinned profiles, and projections under\\nexisting provenance/unitarity rules. A fast path may omit the record\\nonly after proving equivalent evaluation, binding, foreign callability,\\nexceptions, effects, suspension, and identity. Correctness must not\\ndepend on that optimization.\\n\\n#### 8.10.10 Current code and migration\\n\\nThe current code records the gap, not a second normative convention:\\n\\n| Current implementation | Private call path |\\n|---|---|\\n| `yang.python.antlr.prelude` | `py/call-kw` binds arguments and applies the function cell's `:code` to one vector. |\\n| `yang.typescript.antlr.prelude` | `js/call` checks `:js/type :function` and applies `:code` to receiver and argument vector. |\\n| `yang.php.antlr.prelude` | `php/call` applies `:code` to the function cell and argument vector; named/reference handling is separate. |\\n| `test/yang/cross_lang_test.clj` | Demonstrates PHP/TypeScript substrate composition using lookup rewriting and a test-local `js-shaped` wrapper; not proof of this convention. |\\n\\nImplementation proceeds as separately verified changes:\\n\\n1. Supply the shared guest prelude and fixtures for entry discovery,\\n   record validation, and condition transfer, using existing bundled/\\n   linked prelude emitter machinery.\\n2. Add entries to each supported callable constructor and builtin;\\n   preserve source metadata/private code while putting binding and\\n   exception boundaries under the entry. Keep existing source `:call`\\n   safepoint marks on their original source activation, normally the\\n   private body; generated entry/binding boilerplate remains unmarked.\\n   Do not copy a body's source mark onto its entry and count both. A\\n   separately proved instrumentation transformation may relocate a mark\\n   only while preserving its source timing and exactly one activation.\\n3. Route source-resolved calls and callable tests through the convention,\\n   including supported bound methods, callable objects, recursion, and\\n   nested runtime re-entry.\\n4. Integrate explicit export/name projections with module publication\\n   and task-local instantiation; remove test-only language-pair wrappers\\n   from the new conformance paths.\\n5. Pass the acceptance gates before advertising a conforming frontend\\n   revision. Old body signatures must not be mistaken for new entries.\\n\\nA frontend is not conformant while its advertised supported callable\\nkinds need pairwise wrappers. Legacy non-ANTLR frontends gain no claim\\nfrom this proposal; joining later requires the same entry obligations.\\nThere is no implication that current Python, PHP, TypeScript, or a host\\ntarget has completed this migration.\\n\\n#### 8.10.11 Acceptance gates\\n\\nExecute real lowered source bodies, not only synthetic functions with\\nthe expected slot. A harness may compose published exports and namespace\\nprojections explicitly. It must not rewrite call nodes, extract foreign\\n`:code`, or insert test-local language-pair wrappers to make calls work.\\n\\nRequired evidence:\\n\\n- **Local semantics:** existing suites preserve argument order, defaults,\\n  required/extra arguments, keyword errors, receivers, exceptions, and\\n  supported reference/static behavior.\\n- **Direct calls:** each implemented frontend calls every other\\n  conforming frontend through the same mechanism in both directions\\n  with compatible values. Derive the test matrix from admitted\\n  frontends; do not hard-code it into runtime dispatch.\\n- **Higher-order calls:** pass, return, store, recurse, and call back\\n  through received functions. Assert identity and captures without\\n  replacement cells.\\n- **Callable admission:** source data with an entry-spelled property\\n  remains data; profile-constructed descriptors are admitted. Exercise\\n  supported PHP string/array designators both under local resolution and\\n  explicit callable export, preserving required late lookup without\\n  foreign namespace guessing.\\n- **Presence and binding:** distinguish missing from explicit\\n  null/None/undefined; retain duplicate keywords until binding; supply\\n  bound self once; evaluate receiver/spreads/defaults at source-defined\\n  times and counts.\\n- **Dynamic callability:** where supported, an existing instance becomes\\n  eligible after its class gains a call method and ineligible after\\n  deletion. Calls whose arguments change this metadata observe the\\n  correct source-required lookup time without instance rewrapping.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n1154:collab/exception-track/1794930000000-architect-exception-ruling-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:40:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_19\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"rg -n 'exception|outcome|fast path|same continuation|continuation' docs/design/yang.clojure.md docs/design/yang.antlr.md collab/yang-call/*ruling* collab/*/*cell-ref*; nl -ba collab/exception-track/1794920000000-owner-ruling-exception-uniformity.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"rg: docs/design/yang.clojure.md: No such file or directory (os error 2)\\ncollab/yang-call/1794350000000-orchestrator-to-yangpython-cell-ref-evidence.md:26:3. **Audit and record that the remaining sites are safe** (branch on shape, never deref) — closes your half with no code. That's a legitimate outcome; my audit only confirmed the three deref sites, not the nine branch ones.\\ncollab/yang-call/1794340000000-orchestrator-to-ts-php-python-seeds-cell-ref-ruling.md:14:1. **yin.vm cell module** (`src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc` + one interpreter case in `engine.cljc`): additive — a new effect constructor; `cell-profiles` publication grows `authentic?`. No state, seal, continuation, or protocol change; existing effects untouched. Verified repo-wide: no test pins the exact vocabulary set.\\ncollab/yang-call/1794340000000-orchestrator-to-ts-php-python-seeds-cell-ref-ruling.md:14:1. **yin.vm cell module** (`src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc` + one interpreter case in `engine.cljc`): additive — a new effect constructor; `cell-profiles` publication grows `authentic?`. No state, seal, continuation, or protocol change; existing effects untouched. Verified repo-wide: no test pins the exact vocabulary set.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794330000000-architect-cell-ref-ruling-of-record.md:19:5. **Static specialization is an optimization, not a semantic** (astra rule 5): projected-name fast paths may exist only with proof of equivalence under shadowing and higher-order use — never as the routing default.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794330000000-architect-cell-ref-ruling-of-record.md:21:7. **The entry-key (`:yang.call/entry`) admission policy is OUT OF SCOPE** — it stays exactly as landed and gated in D2 (an unadmitted entry-shaped map refuses; pinned clojure_test.clj:2600). \\\"Ordinary map application always remains data\\\" is therefore true for the cell-ref language, not universally; §8.10 documents the entry-key policy as the one declared shape-routed exception.\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794330000000-architect-cell-ref-ruling-of-record.md:25:§8.10 (yang.antlr.md) gains: the `cell/authentic?` vocabulary entry; the position/semantics rule (routing consults the heap for cell-ref-shaped callees); the bearer-copy semantic (rule 4); the bag semantic clarification (a reference stored in data REMAINS a reference — storage changes nothing; what decides is the authentication query at the call); and the entry-key exception note (rule 7). The cell-profiles publication and any module docs listing the vocabulary update accordi