\ndocs/design/yang.antlr.md:4774:9. **Semantic preservation:** observable values, mutation, exceptions,\\ndocs/design/yang.antlr.md:4780:    continuations resume from explicit state; native parser stacks are\\ndocs/design/yang.antlr.md:4782:12. **Honest unsupported outcomes:** unavailable hosts, runtime\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794320000000-architect-cell-ref-two-api-ruling.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:7:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_2\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"cat docs/design/datom.world.md; rg --files -g 'AGENTS.md' -g '*engine.cljc' -g '*clojure.cljc' -g '*conformance.clj' -g '*typescript*' -g '*prelude*' -g '*call*' docs/design src test | head -100\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"---\\\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\\\n---\\\\n\\\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\\\n\\\\n## Core Philosophy\\\\n\\\\nEverything is data.\\\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\\\n\\\\n### Foundational Axioms\\\\n\\\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\\\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\\\n   At system scale, a \\\\\\\"system\\\\\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\\\n\\\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\\\n\\\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\\\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\\\\n\\\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\\\n\\\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\\\n\\\\n## Streams\\\\n\\\\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\\\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\\\\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\\\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\\\n\\\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\\\nthe stream's own.\\\\n\\\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\\\n\\\\n### Host Boundaries\\\\n\\\\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\\\\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\\\\n\\\\n## Architectural Commitments\\\\n\\\\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\\\\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\\\\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\\\\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\\\\n\\\\n## Design Principles\\\\n\\\\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\\\\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\\\\n\\\\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\\\\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\\\\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\\\\n\\\\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\\\\n  ordinary streams.\\\\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\\\\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\\\\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\\\\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\\\\n  auto-index observer.\\\\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\\\\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\\\\n\\\\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\\\\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\\\\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\\\\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\\\\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\\\\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\\\\nretired `:global` tag in\\\\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\\\\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\\\\n\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\\\\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\\\\n\\\\n## Code Style\\\\n\\\\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\\\\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\\\\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\\\\n- Avoid cleverness.\\\\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\\\\n\\\\n## Development Workflow\\\\n\\\\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\\\\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\\\\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\\\\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\\\\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\\\\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\\\\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\\\\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\\\\n  leaves to review.\\\\n\\\\n### Problem Decomposition\\\\n\\\\nIf a problem appears complex:\\\\n1. Decompose it into streams.\\\\n2. Identify invariants.\\\\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\\\\n4. Make causality explicit.\\\\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\\\\n\\\\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\\\\n\\\\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\\\\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\\\\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\\\\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; each orchestrator session has its own append-only log at `orchestrator-logs/<unix-time-stamp>-<brief-orchestrator-descriptive-name>.md` under the repository root.\\\\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.typescript.md\\\\ndocs/design/dao.jing.call-site-classification.md\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj\\\\ntest/yang/php/antlr/prelude_test.cljc\\\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/source_call_test.cljc\\\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/class_prelude_test.cljc\\\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc\\\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/source_call_parser_test.clj\\\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/source_call_fixtures.cljc\\\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_module_test.cljc\\\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc\\\\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_test.cljc\\\\ntest/yang/core/call_test.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/caller.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_module.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/php/antlr/prelude_p9.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/python/antlr/prelude.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/prelude.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc\\\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/call_entry_test.cljc\\\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc\\\\ntest/yang/python/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc\\\\n\\\",\\\"exit_code\\\":0,\\\"status\\\":\\\"completed\\\"}}\\ncollab/yang-clojure/1794320000000-architect-cell-ref-two-api-ruling.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log:9:{\\\"type\\\":\\\"item.completed\\\",\\\"item\\\":{\\\"id\\\":\\\"item_3\\\",\\\"type\\\":\\\"command_execution\\\",\\\"command\\\":\\\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\\\\\"rg -n '8\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\\.10|D2|token-packet|dsg__call_token|descriptor.admission|cell-ref|identity-check|identity|projection|projected|exports|profile' docs/design/yang* src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc test/yang/call/conformance.clj src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc\\\\\\\"\\\",\\\"aggregated_output\\\":\\\"src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:3:   §8.10). This namespace emits the same pure definitions into bundled\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:22:  '[;; Descriptor projection remains structural after callable admission.\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:26:         (if (= (get callable :type) :cell-ref)\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:146:(def ^:private public-exports\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:168:(def module-exports\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:169:  \\\\\\\"Public module exports (private helper definitions stay internal).\\\\\\\"\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:170:  public-exports)\\\\nsrc/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:182:                     :exports module-exports\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:3:   yang.antlr.md section 8.10.11): a donor program's named export reaches a\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:15:   inserted, and the projected value is the donor's own callable value\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:16:   (identity survives).\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:90:  [packet exports]\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:97:                        exports)\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:102:  \\\\\\\"Each admitted frontend's profile, as data:\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:109:   - `:unit`: packet and projected exports to the lowered unit its\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:160:    :unit (fn [packet exports] (clj-comp/lower-unit packet exports)),\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:166:               ;; reads each projected name through `clj/global-get`, so\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:169:               ;; projection and shadows it.\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:174:(defn- profile\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:181:  \\\\\\\"The host registry, admitted by every runtime profile that checks it,\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:197:   (let [{:keys [runtime address refusal parse unit deps]} (profile frontend)\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:205:                 :exports '#{body},\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:223:        (distinct (map (comp :runtime profile) frontend-keys))))\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:230:   (u/app (u/v (:run-main (profile frontend)))\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:238:  (let [{:keys [read-export]} (profile (:frontend donor))]\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:246:  \\\\\\\"The caller program: the donor's body run, each name of `exports` read\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:247:   from the donor runtime and projected into `caller-frontend`'s namespace\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:250:  [donor exports caller-frontend caller-source]\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:251:  (let [{:keys [read-export]} (profile (:frontend donor))\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:252:        {:keys [project parse unit run-main]} (profile caller-frontend)]\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:262:                     exports)\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:263:                [(u/app (u/v run-main) (unit (parse caller-source) exports))])))))\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:298:  \\\\\\\"Publish the donor `[frontend source]`, compose its `exports` into the\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:302:  [[donor-frontend donor-source] exports [caller-frontend caller-source]]\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:304:        render (:render (profile caller-frontend))]\\\\ntest/yang/call/conformance.clj:305:    (agreed (update-vals (run-program donor (compose donor exports caller-frontend\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:35:- [yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md): Universal AST content and projection discipline.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:36:- [yin.vm.semantic.md](./yin.vm.semantic.md): A identity, semantic execution and A→R derivation.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:55:**A is the only code identity.** R is the register image checksum. The\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:57:artifact with no identity in `dao.jing`, no content address, and no\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:73:under the baseline profile. This is not ClojureScript-to-wasm. Toolchain pins\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:79:The mandatory feature profile is **core wasm 1.0 with mutable globals and the original function\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:90:Every extension requires a separate named profile, W0 validation/probe on all target engines and\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:112:- The October amendments to the register and semantic designs make **A the code identity**. R is the register image checksum. Existing `:hash`, `:image`, and offset rows still use R for native compatibility. Do not publish wasm binaries as a new code identity, fetch code by a wasm checksum, or bypass A-to-R admission. Retain access to the Universal AST through existing provenance/addressing.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:135:- Current A identity and A→R admission above govern over the older Rust topology and R-as-identity wording (§§3, 7.5). R remains the exact executable checksum/offset-row token. Neither backend creates an identity or bypasses canonical admission.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:136:- Rust K-D2b uses u32 indices and qualified host limits (§4.2 and §11 K-D2b); wasm keeps 64-bit **wire pc fields**, not a promise of unrestricted native execution. The internal facade checks every pc/operand/attachment against the Rust domain and declared limits before conversion or mutation and returns the same qualified `:host-limit`. W0 records the common accepted profile; tests outside it require declared refusals, never truncation or a second widened Rust Machine. Wide register tests above 65,535 still fit u32.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:146:scalar profile fixes only this lane's supported domain, not all future Rust profiles (questions\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:214:### 2.3 Module imports, exports and deterministic emission\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:233:profile yield byte-identical WAT and assembled `.wasm` binaries across runs and platforms.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:364: :image identity-of-row-containing-resume-pc\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:455:section. The baseline preallocates table capacity (W0 test profile: 65,536 slots) and uses host\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:458:must pass the W0 probe on each runtime and must not change the baseline module feature profile.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:476:derivation/checksum, wasm layout/emitter version and feature profile; R alone is not executable\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:481:target/profile, translator and internal-layout versions in the cache key alongside the admitted A/R\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:501:- **W0 — Oracle and feasibility:** export/extend K0 from CLJS first; freeze admitted images/R/traces/refusals; probe crate wasm32 compilation, linker/profile, internal layout and stream-adapter bounds. Pin deterministic emitter/assembler. No host-service fallback.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:506:- **W5 — Complete lane:** all 23 opcodes and complete W1–W5 full traces on Node, Wasmtime and Wasmer; deterministic binaries and external import/profile audit. Wasmer's formerly W4 full-suite gate is superseded by this owner-directed schedule, not removed.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.wasm.md:528:- **Canonical admission and provenance.** Bare hand images remain useful under the existing raw kernel API; production module execution must retain A derivation verification. Compiled wasm caches are disposable artifacts. They cannot become another authority for code or continuation identity.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:67:6. **Immutable identity.** Pin the full *checking configuration* for each checking result: the checked declaration environment, term graph, compiled kernel artifact, VM/compiler/schema versions, and the runtime trust identity of the closed executable composition the checker runs under (section 5).\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:87:| Hash/identity of canonical terms | Important for performance | Sound structural comparison before trusting cache shortcuts |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:107:#### Checking configuration and runtime trust identity\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:109:A checking result must not be identified by its listed ingredients alone. At run time the checker resolves every name through a fixed lookup chain — lexical bindings, program store, primitives, then the composition-supplied module registry (`resolve-var` in `src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc`) — and a host module is a declared boundary, not a content-addressed one: registration checks the profile, not the code, and a host module's address is nil because the host is where the code already is (`src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc`). Two runs can therefore present identical listed result identifiers while executing different trusted operation implementations.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:114:- The closed set of linked modules and their identities: content addresses for fetched code, and for host modules an explicit **host attestation** — module name, exported symbols, profile records, and the host implementation version the publishing composition answers for. Where content addressing is unavailable, the attestation and version are the identity.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:120:- The configuration is part of result and cache identity; results and cache entries are shared only between runs with the same configuration.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:128:Define tagged logical constructors, projected onto the universal graph/tuple model:\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:140:These are **logical term tags** and may be represented as datom attributes/relationships in the existing UAST. Do not confuse them with Yin's runtime instruction opcodes, even when both use the same data substrate. A translation/projection can use common interned nodes when semantics permit.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:172:A specification change mints a new spec version: it changes the checking-configuration identity (section 5) and invalidates previously issued checking artifacts.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:180:- Tagged term constructors and projections.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:187:**Acceptance:** Terms round-trip through the datom/UAST projection, preserve binding, and cannot reference invalid environment versions.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:204:theorem identity : (P : Prop) → P → P :=\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:210:**Acceptance:** The dependent identity proof is accepted and malformed variants are rejected — each judged against exactly the judgments the frozen Gate S specification defines, with rejections citing the judgment violated. This is not yet a complete CIC kernel; acceptance covers the v1 spec fragment and nothing beyond it.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:221:- Sound memoization keyed to **every judgment input**: the compared terms, the local typing context including its local definitions, the semantic substitution environment for open terms, the global environment identity, and the reduction policy (transparency and admitted unfoldings). Identical de Bruijn syntax under different local definitions can reduce differently, so term syntax plus global environment alone is not a sound key. In v1, conversion caching is restricted to closed terms under a pinned global environment and reduction policy; caching open-term results additionally requires the full local context and semantic environment in the key.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:263:- Cache checks by checking-configuration identity (section 5) with sound collision/identity handling.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:272:- Implement the exact kernel features required by each target corpus: universe rules, proof irrelevance, inductive computation, quotients, projections, literals, transparency, etc.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:276:**Acceptance:** Publish a precisely bounded subset of Lean exports that Yin.CIC independently rechecks with matching outcomes. Full Lean compatibility is not assumed.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:282:  term.cljc          ; logical terms and projections\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:344:1. Pick a tiny source subset supported by `yang.clojure`: `fn`, application, `if`, `let`, and deterministic data projections.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:363:- How will compiler/VM semantic changes invalidate previous checking artifacts? The mechanism is checking-configuration identity (section 5); the residual decision is the reissue policy for results issued under an older configuration.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.cic.md:378:**Bottom line:** Write the checker in Clojure, compile it using Yang, and run it as a Yin program. Keep the logical checker and the proof term semantics explicit; freeze the trusted code image and environment; audit the Yang/Yin trust boundary; and validate the design with a minimal dependent identity proof before tackling Lean compatibility.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:27:- The parser, elaborator, checker, and any execution adapter are programs. When compiled for Yin, their executable UAST follows the existing VM projection and segment contracts.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:52:The need for an interpretation identity follows from the axiom; the particular closed notation selected for v1 is a product decision.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:118:A content hash is an identity, not a retention mechanism. Publication must retain the referenced artifacts, not merely their hashes.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:126:This is an engineering constraint, not an impossibility theorem. “Community-extensible syntax” here means that ordinary libraries may introduce new concrete notation, fixity, binding forms, or interpretation procedures beyond a frozen profile. A fixed extension mechanism can support bounded combinations, but its effective notation environment still becomes an interpretation input.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:131:| Mathematical surface and a closed grammar | Parsing and lowering can be frozen and audited | New conventions require a new profile or frontend |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:150:| Source admission | Source bytes and explicit request profile | Retained source artifact and request record | Validates encoding and request shape |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:156:Each emitted artifact and each derivation record is content-addressed. A transport cursor or retry identifier is an occurrence coordinate, not the content identity of a term.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:182:Term content is not normalized merely to improve hash sharing. Structural identity and logical definitional equality remain different relations. Any term canonicalization must be specified and tested independently of proof checking.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:188:For executable programs, [the semantic VM contract](./yin.vm.semantic.md) remains authoritative: the canonical register-shaped segment address **A** is the executable code identity. AST row addresses, logical term roots, and source artifact addresses must not be relabeled as A.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:190:No Tao-specific instruction, evaluator mode, or alternative segment identity is introduced.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:214:The first mathematical example is dependent identity: the proposition \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\Pi(P:\\\\\\\\mathrm{Prop}).\\\\\\\\,P\\\\\\\\to P\\\\\\\\), with the explicit proof \\\\\\\\(\\\\\\\\lambda(P:\\\\\\\\mathrm{Prop}).\\\\\\\\,\\\\\\\\lambda(h:P).\\\\\\\\,h\\\\\\\\). Its validity comes from Gate S’s predicative theory, not from resemblance to Lean notation.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:230:The notation profile must freeze token boundaries, punctuation, comments, qualified-name syntax, universe spelling, and any ASCII aliases before parser acceptance. These choices cannot remain host-library defaults.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:236:The notation profile pins Unicode handling and the identifier repertoire. Identifier normalization is explicit: v1 requires the selected normalized spelling and rejects nonconforming identifiers instead of silently changing them. Original bytes remain retained even on rejection.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:273:- Frontend and notation-profile identity.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:289:Cache identity includes the inputs and configuration of the cached stage. An earlier source-level receipt cannot certify a new interpretation merely because the final term happens to share an address. Any reuse of a narrower kernel result must remain distinguishable from evidence connecting written notation to that term.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:291:Addresses establish content identity. They do not prove that the implementation is correct, that a host attestation is trustworthy, or that artifacts remain available.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:301:Occurrence identity includes origin, root, and structural path. A content address alone is insufficient: repeated identical subexpressions can have different source locations and binding contexts.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:338:The explicit statement renderer is itself an interpretation dependency for human review. Its identity must be recorded when its output is used as audit evidence. It must not hide arguments or restore ambiguous notation by default.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:368:Publishing a new frontend or notation profile does not mutate an existing profile. Reinterpreting old source under the new profile produces new evidence. A consumer explicitly chooses which interpretation it accepts.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:379:| E3 — Checking composition | Candidate medium connected to the CIC checker under pinned configurations | Dependent identity accepted and specified invalid variants rejected, after Gate S and the required CIC milestones |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:411:- Interpretation identity accompanies checking identity.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.tao.md:422:The notation profile must settle remaining concrete spellings before E0 closes. Wire schemas and event layouts require a focused review against existing row and stream contracts; this note does not add speculative slots to canonical VM structures.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:9:progress through the linked inclusion profile, dynamic scope, namespaces,\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:41:  php-src selection, the full permutation corpus and the linked profile (P4\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:72:revision and profile keys; the final naming at acceptance is the owner's.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:92:parsing with three-host execution, the linked-profile matrix of bundled on\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:193:- **The dynamic-scope profile**: a function whose body contains\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:236:  and value; objects by property, or by identity for `===`.\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:361:scope, so it forces the dynamic-scope profile on its function. It is refused\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:599:| 6  | `$x =& $x`, or the same slot on    | no-op: the identity check precedes |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1462:- **Worker**: `yang.antlr.cst/make-worker` unchanged, profile `{:grammar-id\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1500:under a ticks profile only (P7-b).\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1533:|            | `$$x` (dynamic profile, P9),   | writes through `php/assign`    |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1664:|                          | `extract` (dynamic profile);         |            |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1772:### 10.1 Value encoding (runtime profile data)\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1880:  function is on the dynamic-scope profile (at top level it is `$GLOBALS`).\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1912:   without concrete segment pins\\\\\\\"), not solved here (owner decision B8.10).\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1929:  nondeterministic: the profile offers a deterministic **safepoint-count\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:1997:|       | profile, CST export of modes     | a heredoc with indentation        |\\\\ndocs/design/yang.php.md:2015:|       | and linked profiles, the         | every host; float-address goldens 