{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba src/cljc/yang/core/uast.cljc | sed -n '1,90p'; nl -ba src/cljc/yang/tails.cljc; nl -ba docs/design/yang.antlr.md | sed -n '3838,3955p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns yang.core.uast\n     2\t  \"Universal AST constructors shared by every language lowering and prelude. Every\n     3\t   function returns a canonical map node (`:literal`, `:variable`, `:lambda`,\n     4\t   `:application`, `:if`, `:vm/current-continuation`); nothing here adds a\n     5\t   node type.\n     6\t\n     7\t   `sexp->uast` reads a small s-expression notation into those same nodes so\n     8\t   the prelude can be written legibly:\n     9\t\n    10\t     symbol            -> :variable\n    11\t     (fn [params] b)   -> :lambda\n    12\t     (if t c a)        -> :if\n    13\t     (do a b ... z)    -> sequencing through one-parameter lambdas\n    14\t     (let [x v ...] b) -> nested immediately applied lambdas\n    15\t     (quote x)         -> :literal x\n    16\t     (%capture)        -> :vm/current-continuation\n    17\t     (yin/def k v)     -> the definition application\n    18\t     (f a ...)         -> :application\n    19\t     any other value   -> :literal\"\n    20\t  (:require\n    21\t    [yang.tails :as tails]\n    22\t    [yin.vm :as vm]))\n    23\t\n    24\t\n    25\t(defn lit\n    26\t  [x]\n    27\t  {:type :literal, :value x})\n    28\t\n    29\t\n    30\t(defn v\n    31\t  [sym]\n    32\t  {:type :variable, :name sym})\n    33\t\n    34\t\n    35\t(defn lam\n    36\t  [params body]\n    37\t  {:type :lambda, :params (vec params), :body body})\n    38\t\n    39\t\n    40\t(defn app\n    41\t  [op & args]\n    42\t  {:type :application, :operator op, :operands (vec args)})\n    43\t\n    44\t\n    45\t(defn if-node\n    46\t  [t c a]\n    47\t  {:type :if, :test t, :consequent c, :alternate a})\n    48\t\n    49\t\n    50\t(def capture {:type :vm/current-continuation})\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t(defn def!\n    54\t  \"`(yin/def key value)`: key is a literal symbol.\"\n    55\t  [key value]\n    56\t  (app (v 'yin/def) (lit key) value))\n    57\t\n    58\t\n    59\t(def discard\n    60\t  \"The parameter a sequencing lambda binds and ignores. `%` cannot begin a\n    61\t   Python identifier, so it never shadows a guest name.\"\n    62\t  '%_)\n    63\t\n    64\t\n    65\t(defn then\n    66\t  \"Evaluate `a` for effect, then `b`.\"\n    67\t  [a b]\n    68\t  (app (lam [discard] b) a))\n    69\t\n    70\t\n    71\t(defn seq-nodes\n    72\t  \"Evaluate every node in order; the value is the last one's.\"\n    73\t  [nodes]\n    74\t  (reduce (fn [acc node] (then acc node)) (first nodes) (rest nodes)))\n    75\t\n    76\t\n    77\t(defn let1\n    78\t  [sym value body]\n    79\t  (app (lam [sym] body) value))\n    80\t\n    81\t\n    82\t(defn sexp->uast\n    83\t  [form]\n    84\t  (cond\n    85\t    (symbol? form) (v form)\n    86\t    (seq? form)\n    87\t    (let [[head & args] form]\n    88\t      (case head\n    89\t        fn (lam (first args) (sexp->uast (second args)))\n    90\t        if (if-node (sexp->uast (first args))\n     1\t(ns yang.tails\n     2\t  \"Tail marks over the Universal AST, shared by every stage that builds or\n     3\t   rewrites a tree. A tail call pushes no frame on the VMs that honour the\n     4\t   mark, so a mark is only sound for the tree it was computed over: a stage\n     5\t   that wraps a body must strip every mark and recompute them over the whole\n     6\t   derived tree, or a wrapped body's old tail calls would skip the wrapper.\"\n     7\t  (:require\n     8\t    [yin.vm :as vm]))\n     9\t\n    10\t\n    11\t(defn mark-tails\n    12\t  \"Set `:tail? true` on every application in tail position of a lambda body,\n    13\t   through `if` branches. Definitions are never marked. Lowering relies on\n    14\t   this for loops, which are self-applied lambdas: the recursive call and the\n    15\t   sequencing lambda around it are both tail calls, so an iteration grows no\n    16\t   continuation on the VMs that honour the mark.\"\n    17\t  [node]\n    18\t  (letfn [(walk\n    19\t            [node tail?]\n    20\t            (case (:type node)\n    21\t              :lambda (assoc node :body (walk (:body node) true))\n    22\t              :if (assoc node\n    23\t                         :test (walk (:test node) false)\n    24\t                         :consequent (walk (:consequent node) tail?)\n    25\t                         :alternate (walk (:alternate node) tail?))\n    26\t              :application\n    27\t              (let [definition? (= 'yin/def (:name (:operator node)))\n    28\t                    node (assoc node\n    29\t                                :operator (walk (:operator node) false)\n    30\t                                :operands (mapv #(walk % false)\n    31\t                                                (:operands node)))]\n    32\t                (if (and tail? (not definition?))\n    33\t                  (assoc node :tail? true)\n    34\t                  node))\n    35\t              node))]\n    36\t    (walk node false)))\n    37\t\n    38\t\n    39\t(defn strip-tails\n    40\t  \"`node` with every `:tail?` mark removed, at every depth and under every\n    41\t   node type the row grammar knows, so `mark-tails` starts from nothing.\"\n    42\t  [node]\n    43\t  (let [slots (get vm/semantic-bytecode-grammar (:type node))]\n    44\t    (reduce (fn [n [field kind]]\n    45\t              (case kind\n    46\t                :node (update n field strip-tails)\n    47\t                :nodes (update n field #(mapv strip-tails %))\n    48\t                n))\n    49\t            (dissoc node :tail?)\n    50\t            slots)))\n    51\t\n    52\t\n    53\t(defn remark-tails\n    54\t  \"Strip every tail mark and recompute them over the whole of `node`.\"\n    55\t  [node]\n    56\t  (mark-tails (strip-tails node)))\n  3838\t#### 8.10.7 Normal return and escaping conditions\n  3839\t\n  3840\tNormal return is the value of ordinary Yin application. There is no\n  3841\textra return continuation, completion packet around each result, or new\n  3842\tlanguage-specific return instruction.\n  3843\t\n  3844\tAn escaping source exception invokes the supplied raise exit with:\n  3845\t\n  3846\t```clojure\n  3847\t{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown\n  3848\t :yang.call/value  original-thrown-value}\n  3849\t```\n  3850\t\n  3851\tThis is the argument to an exceptional continuation transfer, not an\n  3852\tordinary return. Returning an identical-looking map returns normal data.\n  3853\tLikewise, a source program throwing a map with these keys throws that\n  3854\tmap as the **payload of a new condition**; its shape is never evidence\n  3855\tthat it is already a condition to forward. No engine-effect value is\n  3856\tsynthesized.\n  3857\t\n  3858\tProtocol failures use qualified reasons such as\n  3859\t`:yang.call/not-callable`, `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`, and\n  3860\t`:yang.call/unsupported-binding`, with plain evidence. The caller maps\n  3861\tthem to a documented local exception form retaining the complete\n  3862\toriginal condition. Callee argument-binding failures retain the actual\n  3863\tcallee exception under `:yang.call/thrown`. These reasons do not extend\n  3864\t`dao.stream`'s outcome algebra.\n  3865\t\n  3866\tThe receiving caller interprets an escaping exception as follows:\n  3867\t\n  3868\t1. A payload valid as an exception in its own runtime retains that\n  3869\t   runtime's ordinary catching and identity behavior.\n  3870\t2. A runtime permitting arbitrary thrown values, such as JavaScript,\n  3871\t   may deliver the original payload directly.\n  3872\t3. A runtime requiring exception objects, such as Python or PHP,\n  3873\t   represents an otherwise foreign payload with its own\n  3874\t   `ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and original\n  3875\t   payload. Catch-all catches this local object. Typed source catches\n  3876\t   match its local hierarchy, never guessed foreign class names.\n  3877\t\n  3878\t`ForeignException` is a proposed prelude-owned type, not a VM type.\n  3879\tUnchanged rethrow propagates the original condition without adding\n  3880\twrappers. Raising a new exception creates a new condition. Crossing back\n  3881\tinto a runtime recognizing the original exception restores its original\n  3882\ttype and identity. Formatting a foreign payload must not implicitly\n  3883\tinvoke a foreign method.\n  3884\t\n  3885\tThe runtime associates its generated wrapper's identity with the retained\n  3886\tcondition for the wrapper's entire lifetime, separate from source-writable\n  3887\tattributes. The association normally lives in private wrapper-cell\n  3888\tcontents, or an equivalent task-owned identity structure with the same\n  3889\tlifetime. It survives storing or returning the wrapper after the creating\n  3890\tcall/catch activation has ended, and remains recoverable when that same\n  3891\twrapper is rethrown in another activation. Temporary catch state alone is\n  3892\tinsufficient. Reachability/reclamation must retain the condition and its\n  3893\tpayload while the wrapper is live, without an ambient registry.\n  3894\t\n  3895\tThe runtime must not detect wrappers or unwrap arbitrary maps by their\n  3896\tkey names. JavaScript may expose raw payloads for\n  3897\t`:yang.call/thrown`; its local exception for a protocol failure must still\n  3898\tretain that failure's qualified condition. Rethrowing the same generated\n  3899\texception forwards that complete condition, including protocol reasons;\n  3900\tthrowing an unrelated value creates a new `:yang.call/thrown` condition.\n  3901\tSaved activation state refers to this identity-owned association; exiting\n  3902\tor restoring a catch does not destroy it.\n  3903\t\n  3904\tVM application/custody defects and host implementation failures remain\n  3905\toperational failures. Do not catch them broadly and relabel them as\n  3906\tsource exceptions. Existing explicit stream/module refusals keep their\n  3907\tsource mappings under the relevant profiles.\n  3908\t\n  3909\t#### 8.10.8 Cleanup, re-entry, and suspension\n  3910\t\n  3911\tEach activation establishes its own exception bridge and saves its\n  3912\tcallee runtime's dynamic context. Binding, body, and exit run under that\n  3913\tbridge. Before invoking the caller's raise exit, let source catches and\n  3914\tcleanup complete and restore saved handler/depth/frame state. Normal\n  3915\treturn restores it too. A source `finally` replacing a pending return\n  3916\tor exception does so before common propagation under its own rules.\n  3917\t\n  3918\tIn A-to-B-to-A calls, the innermost activation must not jump to an older\n  3919\tA handler past B. Its entry bridge is the boundary: inner A cleanup\n  3920\tprecedes B cleanup, which precedes outer A handling. Every activation\n  3921\towns its saved state and raise exit, without a single current-foreign-\n  3922\thandler cell or ambient dispatcher. Unchanged rethrow preserves the\n  3923\tinner exception as specified in section 8.10.7.\n  3924\t\n  3925\tCleanup runs once per actual exit. Resuming a suspended VM transition\n  3926\tmust not repeat parameter binding, argument evaluation, defaults,\n  3927\treference acquisition, or completed cleanup. The record, continuations,\n  3928\tand explicit runtime state use the existing continuation machinery and\n  3929\tits admission restrictions.\n  3930\t\n  3931\tA generator or asynchronous function returns its source-defined object.\n  3932\tSuspension on a stream effect keeps the activation; it is not a new\n  3933\tcall. A later generator resume or scheduled job has a new activation and\n  3934\tvalid bridge, never the raise exit of a call that already returned.\n  3935\tUnrestricted continuation escape across foreign frames is not implicitly\n  3936\tsupported: a claiming profile must define and test restoration and\n  3937\treference lifetimes for every crossed frame.\n  3938\t\n  3939\tInvoking a function-valued argument is internal Yin computation.\n  3940\tHost-initiated callbacks remain stream events under\n  3941\t`datom.world.abi.md`; this convention adds no host callback facility.\n  3942\t\n  3943\t#### 8.10.9 Publication, visibility, and reproducibility\n  3944\t\n  3945\tShared callability creates no common global scope. An import or\n  3946\tcomposition explicitly binds exports in the receiving source namespace\n  3947\tunder its normal name/module rules. Missing names keep their source\n  3948\terrors. No fallback searches other frontends, the filesystem, a host\n  3949\tpackage registry, or ambient globals.\n  3950\t\n  3951\tLinked modules publish code and immutable specifications. Their bodies\n  3952\tinstantiate function objects and common entries in the importing task,\n  3953\tas section 8.5.6 requires. Install children must not export live function\n  3954\tcells. A higher-order result is an ordinary runtime value, not a new\n  3955\tlinker publication on each call.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
