{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"tail -100 collab/exception-track/1794332020000-architect-value-path-refusal-shape.codex-astra.stdout.log | cut -c 1-1600; cat .git/HEAD; nl -ba src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc | sed -n '970,1015p;1160,1195p'\"","aggregated_output":"\nVM application/custody defects and host implementation failures remain\noperational failures. Do not catch them broadly and relabel them as\nsource exceptions. Existing explicit stream/module refusals keep their\nsource mappings under the relevant profiles.\n\nhandler/depth/frame restoration BEFORE the transfer; a later generator resume never\nreuses a raise exit belonging to a completed call.\n\n**R2 — foreign reception and retained-condition lifetime (python, php non-compliant\ntoday).** On receiving a thrown condition: (1) a payload valid in the receiving runtime\nkeeps its ordinary catching and identity behavior — delivered UNCHANGED (python raising\nan already-local exception raw is compliant; python raising an OTHERWISE-FOREIGN payload\nraw at :1034 is not); (2) js may deliver the otherwise-foreign payload raw; (3) python\nand php represent an otherwise-foreign payload with their own generated\n`ForeignException`, retaining the complete condition and original payload — catch-all\ncatches it, typed catches match the LOCAL hierarchy, never guessed foreign class names,\nand formatting it never implicitly invokes foreign methods. Retention rides the generated\nexception's IDENTITY for its ENTIRE LIFETIME, outside source-writable attributes, with no\nambient registry (design :3878, :3885). Raising the whole envelope (php :2384) or an\notherwise-foreign raw payload (python :1034) is non-compliant.\n\n**R3 — protocol failures retain and forward the condition (ALL FOUR frontends; v2\nwidened per astra §1 / sol §1).** `:yang.call/not-callable`, `:yang.call/invalid-invocation`,\n`:yang.call/unsupported-binding` (fully qualified) map to a documented local exception\nform retaining the complete original condition — qualified reason AND plain evidence — so\nan unchanged rethrow FORWARDS the original condition without a new wrapper. js discards\nthe condition into fresh text (:1627); python builds a generic TypeError (:1046); clojure\nretains it as catch data (:1306) but public escape reclassifies through unconditional\n`yang.call/thrown` (:1088); php constructs a fresh Error for not-callable and raises the\nenvelope otherwise (:2384), and its source rethrow rejects that envelope (:4085). All\nfour are in scope, reception AND outbound rethrow.\n\n**R4 — clojure unifies or proves equivalence.** The intra-unit outcome-token channel\n(clojure.cljc:1115, :1125-1133) may remain an optimization only with equivalence proof\nacross caught/uncaught throws, protocol rethrow, binding failures, and higher-order\nre-entry (§8.10.9's bar) — a single passing example is insufficient. Public\nprotocol-rethrow repair (R3) does not wait for this decision. Clojure's restricted catch\nsurface (single catch, no `finally`) is a source-semantics decision this ruling does not\nchange.\n\n**R5 — no new shape-routing.** Per the scope bounds above; plus: forged or\nsource-writable attributes on a wrapper-looking value never authenticate a retained\ncondition.\n\n## The baseline (audit of record)\n\nThe gpt-6.1-sol audit (collab/yang-python/1794900000000, verdict DIVERGENT, HEAD\ne9a0b430) is the baseline. Citation corrections of record from the review round: js\nfollows its GUEST continuation handler stack (js.rt/ctx, prelude.cljc:1704), not the host\n\ncodex\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-10 17:35:19 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-11 00:35:19 ICT\n\n**Ruling: (B).** The entry-absent library value path produces a `:yang.call/thrown` condition whose value is the `:yang.call/not-callable` map:\n\n```clojure\n{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown\n :yang.call/value\n {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/not-callable\n  :yang.call/data {:yang.call/callee f}}}\n```\n\nAt site #7, `invoke-value` executes a source-level `throw` of a condition-shaped map. That map is not an authenticated retained condition, so [§8.10.7](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3838) requires the boundary to treat it as the payload of a new thrown condition. The green protocol rows are consistent with this: their refusals travel through `yang.call/transfer` to a real raise exit as protocol conditions. The value path has no such transfer.\n\nThis does not violate R5: the compiler’s boundary constructs the outer condition; no source-writable key authenticates the inner map. Nor does it break R3: this is first construction from a thrown value, not forwarding an unchanged retained protocol condition. R3 continues to require an already-retained protocol condition to be forwarded intact. The token remains exclusively a provenance mechanism for that distinction.\n\n**Minimal fix instruction:**\n\n- Keep the site #7 `:else` in [clojure.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2799) as a throw of the unmarked `yang.call/condition` map. Correct the adjacent comment: the boundary wraps that thrown value and authenticates the **new outer condition**; the library does not cause the inner map to be stamped. Do not use `(f)`, which re-enters the dispatcher.\n- In [clojure_test.clj](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2599), update the five affected assertions at lines 2599, 2619, 2635, 2736, and 2766 in both lanes to expect the outer `:yang.call/thrown` and inspect the inner `:yang.call/not-callable` and its callee evidence. Preserve the error-tag, termination, and budget assertions. The entry-present admission assertions remain raw protocol refusals.\n- Retain the already-directed token equality, token stamping at sites #1–#6, and `remark-tails` changes. Run the affected tests and the protocol and shape conformance rows after the fix.\n\nNo implementation changes were made.\ntokens used\n37,191\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-11-10 17:35:19 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-11-11 00:35:19 ICT\n\n**Ruling: (B).** The entry-absent library value path produces a `:yang.call/thrown` condition whose value is the `:yang.call/not-callable` map:\n\n```clojure\n{:yang.call/reason :yang.call/thrown\n :yang.call/value\n {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/not-callable\n  :yang.call/data {:yang.call/callee f}}}\n```\n\nAt site #7, `invoke-value` executes a source-level `throw` of a condition-shaped map. That map is not an authenticated retained condition, so [§8.10.7](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/docs/design/yang.antlr.md:3838) requires the boundary to treat it as the payload of a new thrown condition. The green protocol rows are consistent with this: their refusals travel through `yang.call/transfer` to a real raise exit as protocol conditions. The value path has no such transfer.\n\nThis does not violate R5: the compiler’s boundary constructs the outer condition; no source-writable key authenticates the inner map. Nor does it break R3: this is first construction from a thrown value, not forwarding an unchanged retained protocol condition. R3 continues to require an already-retained protocol condition to be forwarded intact. The token remains exclusively a provenance mechanism for that distinction.\n\n**Minimal fix instruction:**\n\n- Keep the site #7 `:else` in [clojure.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2799) as a throw of the unmarked `yang.call/condition` map. Correct the adjacent comment: the boundary wraps that thrown value and authenticates the **new outer condition**; the library does not cause the inner map to be stamped. Do not use `(f)`, which re-enters the dispatcher.\n- In [clojure_test.clj](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-r3-orch/test/yang/clojure_test.clj:2599), update the five affected assertions at lines 2599, 2619, 2635, 2736, and 2766 in both lanes to expect the outer `:yang.call/thrown` and inspect the inner `:yang.call/not-callable` and its callee evidence. Preserve the error-tag, termination, and budget assertions. The entry-present admission assertions remain raw protocol refusals.\n- Retain the already-directed token equality, token stamping at sites #1–#6, and `remark-tails` changes. Run the affected tests and the protocol and shape conformance rows after the fix.\n\nNo implementation changes were made.\nref: refs/heads/master\n   970\t  \"Whether a sequence of [target init] pairs or the body after them can fail,\n   971\t   each init seeing the targets before it.\"\n   972\t  [env pairs body]\n   973\t  (loop [env env, ps pairs]\n   974\t    (if (empty? ps)\n   975\t      (any-failing? env body)\n   976\t      (let [[target init] (first ps)]\n   977\t        (or (failing? env init)\n   978\t            (recur (with-locals env (binder-symbols target)) (rest ps)))))))\n   979\t\n   980\t\n   981\t(defn- failing?\n   982\t  \"True when evaluating `form` can produce an error outcome: a throw, or a\n   983\t   call that returns an outcome (a guest call or an adapted primitive).\"\n   984\t  [env form]\n   985\t  (cond\n   986\t    (seq? form)\n   987\t    (if (empty? form)\n   988\t      false\n   989\t      (let [[head & ops] form]\n   990\t        (cond\n   991\t          (contains? #{'quote 'fn} head) false\n   992\t          (= 'throw head) true\n   993\t          (= 'try head) true\n   994\t          (= 'let head) (let [[bindings & body] ops]\n   995\t                          (scoped-failing? env (partition 2 (expand-bindings bindings env)) body))\n   996\t          ;; the raw outcome of one guest call is a value, not a failure\n   997\t          (= 'yin.k/outcome-of head) (do (when-not (and (= 1 (count ops)) (seq? (first ops)))\n   998\t                                           (reject! \"yin.k/outcome-of takes one call\" {:form form}))\n   999\t                                         (when (any-failing? env (first ops))\n  1000\t                                           (reject! \"yin.k/outcome-of operands cannot fail\"\n  1001\t                                                    {:form form}))\n  1002\t                                         false)\n  1003\t          (= 'dsg__let head) (let [[bindings & body] ops]\n  1004\t                               (scoped-failing? env (partition 2 bindings) body))\n  1005\t          (= 'loop head) (let [[bindings & body] ops]\n  1006\t                           (scoped-failing? env (partition 2 bindings) body))\n  1007\t          (contains? control-heads head) (failing? env (expand-control env head ops))\n  1008\t          (contains? lowered-specials head) (any-failing? env ops)\n  1009\t          :else (case (call-kind env head)\n  1010\t                  (:guest :adapted) true\n  1011\t                  :macro (do (check-macro-operands! env head ops) false)\n  1012\t                  (any-failing? env ops)))))\n  1013\t    (or (vector? form) (set? form)) (any-failing? env form)\n  1014\t    (map? form) (any-failing? env (concat (keys form) (vals form)))\n  1015\t    :else false))\n  1160\t            :macro (do (check-macro-operands! env head ops) (ok-form form))\n  1161\t            :keyword (lower-operands env (vec ops)\n  1162\t                                     (fn [vs] (ok-form (rebuild form (cons head vs)))))\n  1163\t            :native (lower-operands env (vec ops)\n  1164\t                                    (fn [vs] (ok-form (rebuild form (cons head vs)))))\n  1165\t            :adapted (lower-operands env (vec ops)\n  1166\t                                     (fn [vs] (rebuild form (cons (get (adapters env) head) vs))))\n  1167\t            :guest (lower-operands env (into [head] ops)\n  1168\t                                   (fn [vs] (rebuild form vs)))))))\n  1169\t    (or (vector? form) (set? form) (map? form))\n  1170\t    (if (failing? env form)\n  1171\t      (cond\n  1172\t        (vector? form) (lower-operands env form (fn [vs] (ok-form (v